(window.LING406_MODERN_PACKS=window.LING406_MODERN_PACKS||{})["m3-simple"]={"id":"simple","label":"Simpler · answer first, one step per sentence","revision":"ling406-modern-m3-simple-2","scripts":{"m3-reading1-p001":[],"m3-reading1-p002":[{"text":"The first thing to remember about **phonemes** is that phonemes are **not letters**. This is because a phoneme is a category of speech sound, and a letter is a written symbol. A writing system stands for a language. But its symbols do not have to match the sound categories one to one.","children":[]},{"text":"One letter can be part of different pronunciations. One phoneme can be written with different spellings. So picking out a letter is a different job from picking out a phoneme.","children":[]},{"text":"The question is about how sounds relate to writing. It is not about how many letters a word has. Since sounds and letters do not match one to one, they are kept apart.","children":[]}],"m3-reading1-p003":[{"text":"Letters are not phonemes either, and scripts differ in how well spelling tells you the sound. This is because in a **mispronouncing contest**, people mispronounced words like COLONEL and PARADIGM on purpose. Reading those words by their usual letter patterns gives the wrong sound.","children":[]},{"text":"This difference between scripts is called **orthographic depth** (how consistently spelling matches pronunciation). **English** is a relatively **deep** script. This is because English has many irregular spellings. So its letters often do not tell you the sound.","children":[]},{"text":"**Finnish** and **Korean** are very **shallow** scripts. Their spelling matches sound very predictably. **Pinyin** for Mandarin and **French** are also fairly regular.","children":[]},{"text":"A deep script is harder to predict, but it is not random. The difference is in the writing system, not in how able its readers are.","children":[]}],"m3-reading1-p004":[{"text":"Finnish children learn to **decode** (figure out a pronunciation from a written word) very quickly. This is because Finnish spelling is very **transparent**, or **shallow** in the terms of the **Orthographic Depth Hypothesis**. Each letter matches one phoneme more or less without ambiguity. So learning one letter-sound match helps with many words.","children":[]},{"text":"Doubled letters mark length. A doubled consonant stands for a **geminated** consonant (a consonant held longer). A doubled vowel stands for a **long vowel**.","children":[]},{"text":"Following a beginning Finnish lesson along with its text makes this clear. After about a minute, a listener gets the idea. Since the matches are so consistent, children can read words they have never seen.","children":[]}],"m3-reading1-p005":[{"text":"Children learn to **decode** more slowly in English than in most other languages that use the Roman alphabet. This is because English is an **outlier** among those languages. Its spelling-sound matches are less predictable.","children":[]},{"text":"**Aro and Wimmer** had children read **pseudowords** (made-up words that follow the spelling and sound patterns of their language) aloud. A pseudoword is not a word the child already knows. So the child has to decode it.","children":[]},{"text":"In **Grade 1**, children learning French, German, Dutch, Spanish, Swedish or Finnish read about 85 to 93 percent correctly. English learners in Grade 1 read only about **half** correctly. English learners got to about 88 percent by Grade 4.","children":[]},{"text":"Since English spelling is harder to predict, English children take longer to learn decoding. They can still decode in the end.","children":[]}],"m3-reading1-p006":[{"text":"In Korean, the angular shape ㄱ goes with **velar** consonants (sounds made with the back of the tongue against the soft palate). This is because Korean writing, **Hangul**, gives similar phonemes similar symbols. That makes Korean even more regular than Finnish.","children":[]},{"text":"The letter ㄱ is g. Doubling it gives ㄲ, which is kk. Adding a stroke gives ㅋ, which is k. All three share the same basic shape.","children":[]},{"text":"Letters are put together into **syllable blocks**. Each block has an **initial** spot, a **medial** spot and sometimes a **final** spot. Since related sounds look related, the letter shapes themselves show how the sounds are organized.","children":[]}],"m3-reading1-p007":[{"text":"You can read GLORP, SPLING, JONK, FEESH and QUISP aloud even though they are not English words. This is **decoding** in action. This is because each **pseudoword** follows enough English spelling patterns to build a believable pronunciation.","children":[]},{"text":"You have never stored a pronunciation for these items. So you cannot be remembering them. Instead, you use knowledge that **generalizes** (carries over) from the words you know.","children":[]},{"text":"These items test decoding, not vocabulary or meaning. Since you can read brand-new letter strings, you know more than a list of memorized words.","children":[]}],"m3-reading1-p008":[{"text":"People read nonwords almost as fast and as accurately as real words. This is because decoding happens fairly **naturally**, **automatically** and **implicitly** (without conscious effort).","children":[]},{"text":"**Lupker, Brown and Colombo (1997)** had people read items aloud. They measured **naming time** (how quickly a pronunciation becomes available). They compared pseudowords, here called **nonwords**, with real words.","children":[]},{"text":"Nonwords took only **5 to 12 percent longer** than low-frequency words. The comparison is with low-frequency words, not with all real words. Since the gap is small, decoding is fast and not a slow, deliberate effort.","children":[]},{"text":"A later question is how to describe decoding. Computational models of word reading ask whether it works by **categorical rules** or **probabilistic constraints**. Rules and probabilistic constraints could both produce fast decoding. So both kinds of model must explain it.","children":[]}],"m3-reading1-p009":[{"text":"Some pseudowords can be read in more than one reasonable way. VONTH, PHANGE, STREAD and SOUGH are examples. This is because each one has a spelling pattern that English says in more than one way.","children":[]},{"text":"When you read one, you use spelling-sound patterns from words you already know. Different familiar words can push you toward different answers. So these items have no single agreed pronunciation.","children":[]},{"text":"These items test how readers deal with **ambiguity** (more than one possible reading) in going from spelling to sound. Since more than one answer feels fine, generalization can be **graded** instead of all-or-none.","children":[]}],"m3-reading1-p010":[{"text":"It is hard to imagine a rule set that explains why both /strɛd/ and /strid/ feel acceptable for STREAD. This is because a **categorical rule** gives one output. Readers accept two outputs here.","children":[]},{"text":"**Generalization** is a good way to test what patterns people have learned about a system. This is because a new item cannot have been memorized. So the answer reveals the pattern the reader learned. The **Wug Test** is another example of this kind of test.","children":[]},{"text":"The two readings of STREAD differ only in the vowel. The rest of the word stays the same. A **statistical account** can give each reading its own amount of support. Since both readings feel fine, the learned knowledge is not a single fixed rule.","children":[]}],"m3-reading1-p011":[{"text":"Irregular words are harder to recognize than regular words. This is called the **regularity effect**. This is because some form of decoding is part of normal reading. Decoding an **irregular** (or **inconsistent**) word by the usual patterns gives the wrong sound.","children":[]},{"text":"This is clear for PINT, ACHE and CHOIR. Their usual spelling patterns point to the wrong pronunciation.","children":[]},{"text":"It is less clear for **neighborhoods** (groups of words that share a spelling pattern). ROLL, POLL and TOLL sound alike, but DOLL does not. COOK, TOOK and BOOK sound alike, but SPOOK does not. Since a word's neighbors can disagree, its difficulty depends partly on how they are pronounced.","children":[]}],"m3-reading1-p012":[{"text":"Readers clearly use **sublexical information** (information about units smaller than a whole word), especially to turn spelling into sound. This is because people read nonwords quickly and accurately. Also, words that follow typical spelling-to-sound patterns are easier to read than words that do not.","children":[]},{"text":"That leaves the question of **lexical processes**. **Lexical information** is knowledge of particular words. Nonwords test general patterns. Differences among familiar words can reveal knowledge of particular words. Since decoding explains only part of reading, word knowledge needs its own account.","children":[]}],"m3-reading1-p013":[{"text":"Knowing a word helps you figure out an unclear letter inside it. This is because of **feedback** in the **Interactive Activation Model** of **McClelland and Rumelhart (1981)**. Visual features activate letters. Letters activate possible words such as WEAR, WORK, WORD and WEAK. Then active words send activation back to their letters.","children":[]},{"text":"In a word perception example, blots cover parts of the letters in WORK. The visible features of the last letter fit K and R, but not D. Only K makes a real word, WORK. So WORK sends activation back to K.","children":[]},{"text":"Over time, **K** goes up close to the maximum. **R** goes up a little, then drops back down. The letter **D** stays low. Since K wins gradually, recognition builds up over time.","children":[]}],"m3-reading1-p014":[{"text":"In the **Interactive Activation Model**, known words boost the letters they contain. This is because **feedback** from activation for known words increases the activation for letters in those words.","children":[]},{"text":"Activation goes in both directions. Letters send activation up to words as evidence. Words send activation back down to support their letters. So recognition is **interactive**. It does not only flow upward from features to a finished word.","children":[]},{"text":"Since words boost their own letters, an unclear letter is easier to recognize inside a familiar word.","children":[]}],"m3-reading1-p015":[{"text":"Real words are named faster than nonwords, and frequent words are named faster than rare words. These are two **lexical effects**, the **lexicality effect** and the **word frequency effect**. This is because experience with a particular word makes it faster to say.","children":[]},{"text":"In one naming study, high-frequency words took about 442 ms. Low-frequency words took about 482 ms. Nonwords took about 541 to 552 ms.","children":[]},{"text":"As with **picture naming**, the more times you have seen a word, the faster you can name it. **Balota and colleagues (2004)** used data from the **English Lexicon Project**. Naming time went down steadily as frequency went up. The effect held up over a very large number of observations.","children":[]}],"m3-reading1-p016":[{"text":"Frequency effects do not seem to be just a side effect of word length. This is because frequency still predicted reaction time after length and other variables were included.","children":[]},{"text":"The worry was a **confound** (another variable that changes along with frequency). Frequent words could also be shorter, so length might cause the speedup.","children":[]},{"text":"A **multiple regression analysis** (a method that measures each predictor's effect while including the others) checked this. It used about 3000 words read by 30 young and older adults. The tasks were **lexical decision** (judging whether a letter string is a real word) and **naming**.","children":[]},{"text":"**Objective** and **subjective frequency** had **negative** coefficients in every column. A negative coefficient means more of the predictor goes with less time. So higher frequency went with **faster** responses.","children":[]}],"m3-reading1-p017":[{"text":"The **frequency effect** is much bigger for **exception words** than for **regular words**. This is the **frequency by regularity interaction**, a landmark finding in psycholinguistics. It means experience matters most when a word breaks the usual spelling pattern.","children":[]},{"text":"This is because exception and regular words differ much more at low frequency than at high frequency. **Naming latency** is the time it takes to start saying a word. For **high-frequency** words, both kinds take about 540 ms. For **low-frequency** words, exception words take about 583 ms. Regular words take only about 557 ms.","children":[]},{"text":"An **interaction** means the effect of one factor depends on the other. So the cost of being irregular is not the same for every word. This finding has affected other areas, most immediately **inflectional morphology**.","children":[]}],"m3-reading1-p018":[{"text":"One explanation is that word recognition has **two separate processes**. One is the **sublexical process**, which is decoding. The other is the **lexical process**, which is knowledge of a particular word.","children":[]},{"text":"This explains the interaction because the lexical process works differently by frequency. For **high-frequency (HF)** words, the lexical process is so fast and efficient that decoding barely matters.","children":[]},{"text":"For **low-frequency (LF)** words, the lexical process is less efficient. So the sublexical process shows up. For **nonwords**, there is no stored word. So the sublexical process shows up there too.","children":[]},{"text":"For an LF exception word, decoding gives a regularized answer. That wrong answer competes with the right one, so naming slows down. Since HF words skip that competition, regularity matters little for them.","children":[]}],"m3-reading1-p019":[{"text":"People with **acquired phonological dyslexia** have trouble specifically with reading pseudowords aloud. It is called **acquired** (as opposed to **developmental**) because it comes from brain injury.","children":[]},{"text":"**Cacciopo van Vliet and colleagues (2003)** tested two patients, M.O. and I.B. Both read real words and nonwords aloud.","children":[]},{"text":"For **real words**, both got about 85 to 100 percent right. This held for concrete and abstract words, every word class and both frequencies. For **nonwords**, they gave believable answers much less often, often under half.","children":[]},{"text":"The hardest nonwords had low **grapheme-to-phoneme correspondence** (a weak match between written units and sounds). Those were only about 15 to 20 percent. Since known words were fine and new forms were not, decoding was what broke down.","children":[]}],"m3-reading1-p020":[{"text":"People with **acquired surface dyslexia** have trouble specifically with **irregular words** after brain injury. This is because they read words by the common spelling pattern. So their mistakes tend to be **regularizations**.","children":[]},{"text":"DEAF is read as “deef”, SAID as “sayed”, BROAD as “brode” and SHOE as “show”. ONE is read as “own”, FLOOD as “flude” and GONE as “goan”. STEAK is read as “steek”, SEW as “sue” and SOOT as “suit”.","children":[]},{"text":"Two patients, MP and KT, read regular words, exception words and nonwords. Regular words were about 89 to 100 percent correct. Nonwords were about 95 to 100 percent correct.","children":[]},{"text":"Exception words dropped, most of all at low frequency. KT got 47 percent on high-frequency and 26 percent on low-frequency exceptions. MP got 93 and 73 percent. Since decoding still works, these patients fall back on it.","children":[]}],"m3-reading1-p021":[{"text":"Surface dyslexia and phonological dyslexia form a classic **double dissociation**. This is because the two groups show opposite patterns after brain injury.","children":[]},{"text":"In **acquired surface dyslexia**, irregular words are hard and come out regularized. But **pseudoword reading** is relatively spared.","children":[]},{"text":"In **acquired phonological dyslexia**, pseudowords are hard. But **exception word reading** is relatively spared.","children":[]},{"text":"A double dissociation means one group fails task A but not task B, and another group shows the reverse. Relatively spared does not mean perfect. Since the patterns are opposite, one task cannot simply be harder for everyone.","children":[]}],"m3-reading1-p022":[{"text":"Double dissociations are so powerful because the damage pattern flips from one patient to another. This is because if task A were just harder than task B, damage would hurt A more in everyone. No patient would show the reverse.","children":[]},{"text":"In reading, phonological dyslexia hurts pseudowords more. Surface dyslexia hurts exception words more. So the two tasks must lean on different parts of the reading system.","children":[]},{"text":"You first compare the costs within each patient. Then you compare the two patients' patterns. Two low scores without an opposite pattern are not a double dissociation. Since the pattern reverses, the tasks rely on **separable processes**. A theory is still needed for what those processes are.","children":[]}],"m3-reading1-p023":[{"text":"Double dissociations give evidence that pseudoword reading and irregular word reading use **separable** processes. This is because each dyslexia can come from damage to a different part of **Morton's logogen model**, from **Morton and Patterson (1980)**.","children":[]},{"text":"**Logogens** are stored word units. Visual input logogens recognize written words. Output logogens hold their spoken forms. **Grapheme-phoneme conversion** turns letters into sounds without a stored word.","children":[]},{"text":"**Phonological dyslexia** results from specific damage to the grapheme-phoneme conversion module. Nonwords have no stored word, so they cannot get through.","children":[]},{"text":"**Surface dyslexia** results from damage to input or output logogens, or the connections between them. Then exception words must go through conversion, so they come out regularized. These are parts of a model, not a brain map.","children":[]}],"m3-reading1-p024":[{"text":"**Patterson (1981)** gave early signs that the dual-route idea is not so simple. This is because errors that should come from one route show signs of the other.","children":[]},{"text":"In the dual-route view, a word is read from its stored pronunciation or by **non-lexical grapheme-phoneme translation**. Surface dyslexia errors were thought to come from the non-lexical route. So they should show no word influences.","children":[]},{"text":"**Marcel (1980)** found several word influences in the errors. **Neologisms** (made-up non-words) happened less often than a purely non-lexical process predicts. One patient read incense as “increase”, partly because the c is ambiguous. A purely letter-based error would be “inkense”.","children":[]},{"text":"So there is a **classic dilemma**. Either surface dyslexia mixes the two routes in a more complicated way. Or non-lexical reading is a more complicated route. Models of normal reading ask the same question, as in **Glushko (1979)**. Patterson thought combining normal and patient evidence holds the best promise.","children":[]}],"m3-reading2-p001":[],"m3-reading2-p002":[{"text":"The model proposes two reading routes, because reading has several findings that one route would have trouble explaining. This is because **pseudoword reading** (reading pronounceable made-up words) is fast and automatic. Word reading is faster than that. Reading is fastest for **high-frequency** words, which are words that appear often in language.","children":[]},{"text":"**Regularity** (how consistently a spelling maps onto one pronunciation) also affects accuracy and response time. **Frequency and regularity interact**, so the effect of one depends on the other.","children":[]},{"text":"In **acquired dyslexia** (reading loss after brain damage), pseudoword reading and irregular word reading can be **dissociated**. This means one can be impaired without the other. This is why two separate routes seem needed.","children":[]},{"text":"The model has a **sublexical route** and a **lexical route**. The sublexical route does **grapheme-to-phoneme conversion** (turning written units into sounds), which is also called **decoding**. The lexical route recognizes real words.","children":[]}],"m3-reading2-p003":[{"text":"The Dual Route Cascaded model joins an interactive word route to a separate rule route. **Coltheart and colleagues (2001)** published this model in Psych Review, and it is called the DRC.","children":[]},{"text":"The lexical part of the DRC is just the **Interactive Activation model**. The model has **no implementation of semantics** (meaning). This is because both its **orthographic lexicon** (store of written word forms) and its **phonological lexicon** (store of spoken word forms) are independent of semantics.","children":[]},{"text":"The **Grapheme-Phoneme Rule System** has processing assumptions that are completely different from the interactive part. This is because it works by applying rules to letters, which is a different kind of processing. So the DRC uses two different kinds of computation.","children":[]}],"m3-reading2-p004":[{"text":"The **Grapheme-Phoneme Rule System** works letter by letter, and word frequency does not change how it works. This is because its rules are activated **serially** (one after another), moving through the letters in order.","children":[]},{"text":"The rule system gets **no feedback** from the lexical route. This is because it does not interact with the lexical route, except at one place.","children":[]},{"text":"That place is the **phoneme response buffer** (the shared output stage). Both routes connect to the same output units there. Since both routes use the same output units, they can **compete** to activate different patterns.","children":[]}],"m3-reading2-p005":[{"text":"**GPC rules** map written units onto **phonemes** (sound categories). This is because each rule says which sound a written unit gets. Some rules cover single letters, and others cover several letters or a particular letter context.","children":[]},{"text":"**Context-sensitive rules** only apply when specified surrounding letters or positions are present. This is because the rule depends on what is around the letter.","children":[]},{"text":"The model also needs **meta-rules** (rules about which rules to use) for each level of analysis. This is because more than one rule can apply to the same letters. For example, a **larger-unit rule** takes priority over smaller-unit rules.","children":[]}],"m3-reading2-p006":[{"text":"The GPC rules are often based on the most frequent pronunciation, but they work as fixed rules. This is because the rules follow statistics about spelling and sound, yet each rule is either applied or not, with no in-between.","children":[]},{"text":"The rule for **-OUGH** has weaker statistical support than the rule for **-EIGH**. This is because the pronunciations of OUGH are more divided across alternatives.","children":[]},{"text":"Even so, both are rules, and both behave the same way during processing. This is because the strength of support does not change how a rule works.","children":[]},{"text":"A **probabilistic account** is different. It lets each alternative pronunciation have its own strength of support. A spread of support across many alternatives means more uncertainty.","children":[]}],"m3-reading2-p007":[{"text":"The DRC's simple rules miss many cases, and this was seen as a feature, not a bug. This is because the rules are meant to explain errors people make, not only correct reading.","children":[]},{"text":"The DRC's rules went through several revisions to improve nonword reading. Finer rules could pronounce more exceptions correctly. This is because they would then remove behavior the model is meant to explain.","children":[]},{"text":"The argument is that people do not use such finely tuned rules for the GPC route. This is because if they did, two patterns would not appear. One pattern is **regularization errors** (pronouncing an exception word as if it followed the usual rule) in **surface dyslexia**. The other is a slowdown for low-frequency exception words.","children":[]},{"text":"A **cognitively useful model** should reproduce the relevant failures, not only maximize accuracy. The crucial test is whether the same processing assumptions produce the observed pattern of success, interference and regularization.","children":[]}],"m3-reading2-p008":[{"text":"**Informational encapsulation** means a process does not freely use every source of relevant knowledge. This is because the process is kept apart from some information, such as world knowledge.","children":[]},{"text":"**Parsing strategies** like **late closure** and **minimal attachment** were motivated by syntactic complexity alone. This is because they were meant to work from structure only, without world knowledge.","children":[]},{"text":"The dual-route models are designed to be similarly modular. The GPC route keeps running and gives correct pronunciations for many words. It gives incorrect responses to some exceptions. We can see this when people make **regularization errors** or when they are forced to name **pseudowords** (pronounceable made-up words).","children":[]},{"text":"In dual-route accounts, GPC conversion works without whole-word meaning or lexical feedback. This is because the GPC route is kept apart from them. **Pseudoword naming** exposes this best, because no familiar word entry is available.","children":[]}],"m3-reading2-p009":[{"text":"The DRC can simulate many phenomena in visual word recognition. **Coltheart and colleagues (2001)** tested it on effects such as **frequency**, **lexicality** (whether an item is a word or a nonword), **regularity** and their **interactions**.","children":[]},{"text":"The tested effects also include **lexical decision** (deciding whether a written item is a word) and patterns found in acquired dyslexia.","children":[]},{"text":"Accounting for several effects with one architecture is stronger than fitting a single result. This is because one design explains many results at once.","children":[]},{"text":"Evaluation also means checking which assumptions stay fixed and which change between tasks. Reading aloud, lexical decision and patient performance are related but different tasks. So any adjustment made for one task has to be named as such.","children":[]}],"m3-reading2-p010":[{"text":"The DRC models **lexical decision** by adding decision criteria to its recognition activity. This is because activity alone does not say when to answer yes or no.","children":[]},{"text":"The model first reduces **letter-word inhibition**, which makes the task harder. This is because without that change, the results make no sense.","children":[]},{"text":"A \"yes\" answer happens in two ways. First, any single word with an activity above a threshold of 0.69 gives yes. Second, the total activity over the orthographic input lexicon can exceed a threshold S. S is 10 for cycles 0 to 20, and it drops to 1.98 if lexicon activity is above 0.2 at cycle 20.","children":[]},{"text":"A \"no\" answer happens if neither criterion is met by the deadline D. The deadline is 42 cycles, unless lexicon activity is above 0.112 after 20 cycles. In that case the deadline is set to 48. This is described as nearly twice as fast as the model normally reads aloud.","children":[]},{"text":"The implementation also changes inhibition and uses time-dependent thresholds. This is because these choices help the model fit the data. They add assumptions beyond the basic two-route diagram.","children":[]}],"m3-reading2-p011":[{"text":"The DRC's routes are **modular**, which means each route has its own processing assumptions. This is because the two routes are **informationally encapsulated** from each other.","children":[]},{"text":"Informational encapsulation means no lexical feedback reaches the GPC rules.","children":[]},{"text":"The routes are also **domain-specific**. This means the GPC rules take only letters as input. They pass phoneme activations to the phoneme system.","children":[]},{"text":"Modularity has three related senses. They are restrictions on inputs, rules of computation, and restrictions on communication.","children":[]},{"text":"Modularity is a claim about how the routes operate and communicate. So it says more than that the model has two pathways.","children":[]}],"m3-reading2-p012":[{"text":"**Seidenberg and McClelland (1989)** treated reading skill as emerging from learned mappings among spelling, pronunciation and meaning. This is because they trained a network to learn these mappings.","children":[]},{"text":"Their model used **PDP models** (parallel distributed processing networks). It trained a simple associative network. Orthography is spelling, phonology is sound structure, and semantics is meaning.","children":[]},{"text":"The model studied mappings from orthography to phonology in a single route. Reading skill emerges from learning. It is not a hand-built list of rules and lexical entries.","children":[]},{"text":"A connection between domains represents a learned mapping. It is not an extra stored dictionary entry for each item.","children":[]}],"m3-reading2-p013":[{"text":"In the PDP account, **rules** are generalizations learned from many words. They are not a separate process. This is because the same learning mechanism produces them.","children":[]},{"text":"Whole-word knowledge is **distributed**. Some is stored in orthography-to-phonology mappings, and some is stored in other parts of the system.","children":[]},{"text":"The same learning mechanism can capture patterns shared by many words and information about one familiar word. Distributed means knowledge is spread across the network. The model still has memory for individual words.","children":[]},{"text":"The research strategy differs from dual-route models. It asks whether **domain-general learning** (learning that is not specific to reading) can explain reading. This contrasts with reverse-engineering the system from adult and neuropsychological data.","children":[]}],"m3-reading2-p014":[{"text":"The network produces the **frequency by regularity interaction** without assuming separate processes for frequency and regularity. This is because regular words fit statistical patterns from many other words.","children":[]},{"text":"Exception words do not fit those patterns. This is because they conflict with the many words that follow the pattern.","children":[]},{"text":"Frequency also affects how well words are learned. Highly frequent words are less affected by inconsistency with other words.","children":[]},{"text":"A low-frequency exception has weak item learning and poor support from the main pattern. Since both factors work against it, the disadvantage is larger than either factor alone.","children":[]},{"text":"Both architectures must explain this interaction.","children":[]}],"m3-reading2-p015":[{"text":"**Regularity** is redefined as **consistency**. Consistency depends on how many similar spellings support a pronunciation. Similar spellings that support the same pronunciation are **friends**, and similar spellings that support a different one are **enemies**.","children":[]},{"text":"A **regular consistent** word has many friends and no enemies, such as LATE and DUST. This is because all the similar spellings agree.","children":[]},{"text":"A **regular inconsistent** word has many friends and at least one enemy, such as GAVE and MINT.","children":[]},{"text":"An **ambiguous** word has about equal numbers of friends and enemies, such as DOWN and KNOWN, or POUR and SOUR.","children":[]},{"text":"An **exception** has few if any friends and many enemies, such as HAVE and PINT. PINT has relatively little support compared with its competing neighborhood.","children":[]},{"text":"Friends and enemies describe pronunciation relations among spellings, not meanings. A simple regular or irregular label can hide differences in degree.","children":[]}],"m3-reading2-p016":[{"text":"In principle, the network pronounces nonwords using the same **learned weights** it uses for words. This is because a new spelling activates patterns that overlap with familiar spellings.","children":[]},{"text":"The network generalizes to the new spelling. No stored entry for that nonword is needed.","children":[]},{"text":"The same statistical patterns that give rise to regularity effects also support nonword reading. This is proposed as a way to decode without a separate GPC rule module.","children":[]},{"text":"Novel pronunciations come from applying learned statistical structure. Whether one learned mapping handles both words and new items still has to be checked against the model's performance.","children":[]}],"m3-reading2-p017":[{"text":"Poor nonword accuracy can challenge the theory or the implementation. This is because a failed simulation does not say what caused its failure.","children":[]},{"text":"In the early model, nonword accuracy was not very good. This is a classic case of ambiguity in model evaluation.","children":[]},{"text":"One possibility is that the theory is wrong, so one associative mechanism cannot read nonwords and exceptions. The other possibility is that the model was built badly. Its representations might miss the similarities needed for generalization.","children":[]},{"text":"Improving the implementation while keeping the theory helps tell these apart. A **representational improvement** is a different move from adding a separate nonword route.","children":[]}],"m3-reading2-p018":[{"text":"Nonword reading depends on how sounds are represented in the model. Many studies show that **decoding** (nonword reading) is related to **phonological processing**.","children":[]},{"text":"The early Seidenberg and McClelland model used **Wickelfeatures** (the early model's way of coding speech sounds). These were an unusual phonological representation.","children":[]},{"text":"**Plaut and colleagues (1996)** improved the phonological representations. Their model then performed better on nonwords.","children":[]},{"text":"Changing the representation can improve decoding without adding a symbolic rule system. So at least part of the earlier nonword failure came from the phonological representation.","children":[]}],"m3-reading2-p019":[{"text":"**Generalization** depends on **similarity**. This is because a network applies what it learned to new items that look similar to old ones. The principle is inherited from behaviorism.","children":[]},{"text":"Poor phonological representations, like Wickelfeatures, do not capture which words are similar in sound. This is because they fail to capture the **similarity space** of words in the language.","children":[]},{"text":"**Word-specific information** is more robust to poor representations. This is because it does not depend entirely on generalization.","children":[]},{"text":"A nonword has no item-specific experience. So it depends more heavily on generalization, and poor representations hurt it more.","children":[]},{"text":"The same weakness can affect words and nonwords unequally, without any dedicated nonword mechanism. A weaker shared representation is different from damage to a separate route, and the two locate the explanation differently.","children":[]}],"m3-reading2-p020":[{"text":"**Harm and Seidenberg (1999)** added noise to the phonological representations. Increasing noise makes the sound representations less reliable. The general learning framework stays the same.","children":[]},{"text":"This produced poorer nonword decoding and **relative sparing of word recognition**, which means word recognition was spared compared with nonword decoding.","children":[]},{"text":"The model replicated several key findings from the developmental dyslexia literature. These included a link between the degree of phonological deficit and **phonological dyslexia** (a reading pattern tied to weak sound processing).","children":[]},{"text":"The noise is the manipulated property. Word and nonword performance are outcomes. So the simulation tests a direction, from representation to reading, rather than only seeing a correlation.","children":[]},{"text":"A selective-looking reading deficit can come from degraded shared representations. This is because nonwords are especially dependent on reliable generalization.","children":[]}],"m3-reading2-p021":[{"text":"Damaging semantics produced a **specific impairment on exception words**. A **lesion** here is a controlled change to the computational model, not an injury seen in a patient.","children":[]},{"text":"**Plaut and colleagues (1996)** explored the other end of the dissociation, which is **surface dyslexia** (a reading pattern with trouble on exception words). They built a simplified semantic system into the **triangle model**, which links orthography, phonology and semantics.","children":[]},{"text":"This is because an exception has weak support from typical spelling-sound regularities. Meaning-related support can help produce its learned pronunciation.","children":[]},{"text":"Reducing that support creates a surface-dyslexia-like pattern. This happens without defining a separate exception-word list as the sole explanation.","children":[]},{"text":"Nonword reading can be specifically impaired even though the model has no dedicated nonword mechanism. This is the other side of the same dissociation.","children":[]}],"m3-reading2-p022":[{"text":"The triangle model is not simply the dual-route model renamed. This is because **semantic representations** are directly involved in it, while the dual-route lexicon is defined independently of meaning.","children":[]},{"text":"A later model, **Harm and Seidenberg (2004)**, implemented **featural semantics** (meaning represented as features). It simulated the mapping of spelling to meaning.","children":[]},{"text":"The **OP mapping** (orthography-to-phonology) and the **SP mapping** (semantics-to-phonology) have the same processing assumptions and learning algorithm. Both mappings are **probabilistic** too.","children":[]},{"text":"These pathways interact during learning. This is because the OP pathway develops differently when the SP pathway is present.","children":[]},{"text":"This interactivity and gradedness lets the models capture that **double dissociations** (two impairments that each occur without the other) are never entirely clean. Phonological dyslexics are not perfect on exception words. Surface dyslexics are not perfect on nonwords.","children":[]},{"text":"Multiple pathways alone do not make a model modular. Learning rules, information types and interactions set the accounts apart. The OP and SP mappings use the same general probabilistic assumptions, unlike the DRC's distinct GPC and lexical procedures.","children":[]}],"m3-reading2-p023":[{"text":"**Woollams and colleagues (2007)** tested the prediction of a strong association between semantic ability and exception-word reading. The patients had **semantic dementia** (a progressive condition).","children":[]},{"text":"Patients with lower semantic scores had poorer low-frequency exception reading. The model and patient results match overall, although individual patients vary.","children":[]},{"text":"This links a reading weakness to a broader semantic measure. It is not only a reading-task dissociation.","children":[]},{"text":"On the semantic score scale, values fall from left to right. So the downward trend means that poorer semantics goes with poorer exception reading. It does not mean that better semantics harms reading.","children":[]}],"m3-reading2-p024":[{"text":"Some patients fall outside the general trend. **Woollams and colleagues** would treat them as **statistical outliers** (scores that sit far from the general pattern) when evaluating model fit.","children":[]},{"text":"Patients who are left of the vertical boundary and above the horizontal boundary count as outliers. This means the model misses a few data points.","children":[]},{"text":"These points are treated the way outliers are usually treated. This is because an outlier does not automatically refute the model. An account of graded association does not predict zero variability.","children":[]},{"text":"The real question is whether these cases form a stable separate pattern or arise within a noisy overall association. Judge them by their location relative to the broader group and by repeated measurements.","children":[]}],"m3-reading2-p025":[{"text":"Following patients over time tests whether semantic and reading ability decline together. This is because semantic dementia is progressive, so repeated testing can reveal a trajectory rather than one unusual score.","children":[]},{"text":"In most cases, when a patient's semantic ability declined, the patient's reading ability also declined.","children":[]},{"text":"**Regression to the mean** (unusual scores moving back toward typical values on retesting) is the pattern that idiosyncratic outliers would show.","children":[]},{"text":"Observations from the same person are connected. So movement toward lower scores on both measures is **coupled decline**.","children":[]},{"text":"A one-time exceptional score is less convincing if the patient's later scores follow the general relation.","children":[]}],"m3-reading2-p026":[{"text":"**Coltheart and colleagues (2009)** simulated surface dyslexia with the DRC. They generated 40,000 model patients.","children":[]},{"text":"The model patients varied on two parameters. These were the integrity of the lexical route and the integrity of the GPC rules.","children":[]},{"text":"For each patient in the Woollams and colleagues data, the researchers selected the model run that best fit that patient's data. This produced an excellent fit.","children":[]},{"text":"The selection step is part of how the fit was achieved. So the fit is different from predicting each patient's pattern in advance.","children":[]},{"text":"The close agreement of the lines describes **goodness of fit**. The large search over simulated patients describes **flexibility**.","children":[]}],"m3-reading2-p027":[{"text":"A **better fit** can be less informative when it comes from much greater flexibility. This is because choosing the best match from many combinations can reproduce data without strongly constraining the explanation.","children":[]},{"text":"The issue is not that close agreement is bad in itself. The issue is whether the account predicts the relation from principled assumptions, or fits each case after seeing the outcome.","children":[]},{"text":"Goodness of fit is only one part of model evaluation. A model should explain why the pattern occurs, not only contain a setting that resembles it.","children":[]},{"text":"Comparing theories requires attention to both fit and the freedom that produced it. This does not justify rejecting a model just because its fit is strong.","children":[]}],"m3-reading2-p028":[{"text":"Both models must explain rapid **decoding** of pseudowords (pronounceable made-up words), which is fast and automatic.","children":[]},{"text":"Words that diverge from typical spelling-to-sound mappings are harder to read. This is the **regularity** effect, and it interacts with word frequency.","children":[]},{"text":"Pseudoword reading and **exception word** reading can be dissociated in neuropsychological patients. This is why both models need to explain the dissociation.","children":[]},{"text":"The models compete over explanation, not over whether these reading patterns exist. They agree on the phenomena but differ on mechanisms.","children":[]},{"text":"One account emphasizes specialized routes. The other emphasizes learning across interacting representational systems. A useful comparison holds the phenomenon constant and asks what each model changes or computes to produce it.","children":[]}],"m3-reading2-p029":[{"text":"The two models explain the same patterns with different mechanisms. For pseudoword reading and regularity effects, the triangle model relies on statistical regularities learned over many words.","children":[]},{"text":"The DRC relies on a dedicated **grapheme-phoneme conversion** rule system for those effects. This is because it assumes separate rules for conversion.","children":[]},{"text":"For frequency effects, the triangle model says more exposure to a word leads to better learning for that word.","children":[]},{"text":"The DRC uses distinct lexicons for input and output, separate from the GPC rules. Word-specific exposure therefore affects lexical processing in the DRC.","children":[]},{"text":"For phonological dyslexia, the DRC points to damage in the GPC route. The triangle model points to deficit or damage in phonological processing generally.","children":[]},{"text":"For surface dyslexia, the DRC points to damage in the lexical route. The triangle model points to deficit or damage in semantic processes generally.","children":[]},{"text":"Each row gives competing explanations for the same finding. The two columns are not two names for the same part.","children":[]}],"m3-reading2-p030":[{"text":"The two approaches differ on whether explanations begin with separate reading routes or with broader language associations. **Dual-route models** mostly use **double dissociations** (two impairments that each occur without the other) to argue for separate processes.","children":[]},{"text":"Pseudowords and exception words can each be impaired on their own. So dual-route models treat them as independent processes.","children":[]},{"text":"Models informed by statistical learning reveal associations instead. Phonology matters for learning and using **OP generalizations** (orthography-to-phonology). Semantics matters for words where OP generalization is ambiguous.","children":[]},{"text":"Both dissociations and associations matter. A theory should explain selective weaknesses and the related abilities that vary with them.","children":[]},{"text":"The dyslexia rows of the comparison contrast GPC damage with phonological damage, and lexical-route damage with semantic damage. These are the key theoretical alternatives, not just different labels.","children":[]}],"m3-reading2-p031":[],"m3-morphology-p001":[],"m3-morphology-p002":[{"text":"Morphology is **generative**, which means people can make new words they never memorized. This is because the language has processes for building words. For example, at some point there was no noun \"generative.\" There was only the verb \"to generate.\" English has a process for turning verbs into nouns, and that is **derivational morphology** (changing one kind of word into another kind).","children":[]},{"text":"The focus here is **inflectional morphology** (adding grammatical information to a word). In English, this pretty much means the **plural** and the **past tense**.","children":[]},{"text":"People sometimes stretch these patterns in playful ways. One person wrote \"She found me crying and she crew too and we both crode.\" Crew and crode take irregular-sounding patterns and put them on cry. They are not standard English, but they come from using a pattern on a new word. Since people make forms like these, the open question is whether they use symbolic rules or patterns they learned.","children":[]}],"m3-morphology-p003":[{"text":"People easily put inflections on words they have never heard. **Jean Berko Gleason (1958)** found this with the **wug test**. Children do this too.","children":[]},{"text":"This is because the test uses a made-up word. A child sees a drawing of a made-up creature. The words under it say \"This is a WUG\". Then a second creature appears. The words now say \"Now there is another one. There are two of them\". The child finishes \"There are two\" by filling in the blank. Saying **WUGS** puts the plural on a new noun.","children":[]},{"text":"The word is new, so the child cannot have memorized its plural. That means the child must be using a pattern they learned. The same method works for the **past tense** with made-up verbs.","children":[]},{"text":"Success means people have **productive knowledge** (the ability to apply a pattern to new items). Since nobody heard WUGS before, saying it means extending a pattern.","children":[]}],"m3-morphology-p004":[{"text":"The English past tense has two kinds of forms, **regular** and **irregular**.","children":[]},{"text":"**Regular** verbs add **-ed**. This is because the regular pattern works on almost any verb. It works on real verbs like show-showed, bake-baked and wade-waded. It also works on made-up verbs like blick-blicked, trog-trogged and wumble-wumbled. The made-up verbs check whether people extend the pattern to new words.","children":[]},{"text":"**Irregular** verbs do not add -ed. Examples are go-went, take-took, eat-ate and sleep-slept. Each one has to be learned as an exception. Some irregulars share smaller patterns with other verbs.","children":[]},{"text":"This split should seem familiar from the **dual-route model** of reading. That model also kept a general pattern apart from word-specific knowledge. Since the past tense has both a pattern and exceptions, any theory has to explain both.","children":[]}],"m3-morphology-p005":[{"text":"**Pinker and colleagues** said words and rules are two separate processes. This is the **Words and Rules** account. It says the two use separate representational formats.","children":[]},{"text":"**Regular** verbs get their past tense from a **symbolic rule** (an abstract operation like \"add -ed\"). This is because the rule does not need a stored past tense for each verb. So the rule can work on a brand-new verb.","children":[]},{"text":"**Irregular** verbs are **lexicalized** (stored as part of that word's own entry) in an **associative network**. This is because each irregular past tense is specific to its word. The network keeps word-specific information. Frequency and similarity affect it.","children":[]},{"text":"The account also has to stop a stored irregular from being replaced by a regularization. Since rules and stored words are different kinds of knowledge here, each kind of verb uses its own process.","children":[]}],"m3-morphology-p006":[{"text":"**Pinker and Ullman (2002)** proposed a **dual-route model** for the past tense. It has two routes, a lexicon and a grammar. The input is a word stem like walk or hold, plus a grammatical feature like past tense.","children":[]},{"text":"The **lexicon** looks up stored forms. For hold, it finds held. It does lookup and association. It is used for roots, idioms, irregulars and some regulars. It is part of **declarative memory** (memory for words and facts), in temporo-parietal cortex.","children":[]},{"text":"The **grammar** puts pieces together. For walk, it adds the suffix -ed. It does combination and unification. It is used for phrases, sentences and any regular form. It is part of the **procedural system** (rules and skills), in frontal cortex and basal ganglia.","children":[]},{"text":"A **blocking** link lets a found irregular stop the regular form.","children":[]},{"text":"It is similar to the dual-route reading model because both separate word-specific knowledge from a productive operation. It is different because reading turns spelling into sound. This model changes a stem to match a grammatical feature. It also came from grammar theory, not patient data.","children":[]}],"m3-morphology-p007":[{"text":"The past-tense model and the reading model look alike, but they started from different places.","children":[]},{"text":"The reading model came from patients. This is because it was built on neuropsychological **double dissociations** (two abilities that can each be damaged while the other is spared).","children":[]},{"text":"The past-tense model came from grammar theory. This is because **Pinker and colleagues** had other concerns. **Generative linguistics** at the time was mostly about **categorical, symbolic, rule-based grammars**. These rules often have **exceptions**. So the Words and Rules account keeps the rules apart from the exceptions.","children":[]},{"text":"Since the two models came from different starting points, they look alike but rest on different evidence.","children":[]}],"m3-morphology-p008":[{"text":"The **dual-process model** puts exceptions in a second process so the grammar can stay neat. This is because linguists often just left exceptions out of the grammar. Adding machinery for them would make the grammar **inelegant** (messy and less simple). So the model handles exceptions somewhere else and keeps the grammar **elegant, symbolic and categorical**.","children":[]},{"text":"The second process has every property the rule route does not have. It is **word-specific** (lexical). It is **sensitive to frequency** of occurrence. It is **sensitive to phonological similarity** (how much words sound alike).","children":[]},{"text":"Keep-kept, weep-wept and leap-leapt are an example. These verbs sound alike and change in the same way. So an exception can belong to a small family of similar verbs.","children":[]},{"text":"Since the exceptions now live in their own process, the rule stays simple. The cost is a second mechanism that also needs explaining.","children":[]}],"m3-morphology-p009":[{"text":"**Rumelhart and McClelland (1987)** said the past tense is all **associative learning**. This is because their model has one network that learns every verb, with no \"add -ed\" rule. Regularity is just a pattern the network picks up from many examples. Irregulars are learned in the same network.","children":[]},{"text":"The model works in three steps. First, a **fixed encoding network** turns the sound of the root form into **Wickelfeatures** (units that stand for sound patterns). Second, a **pattern associator** with **modifiable connections** maps those features onto past-tense Wickelfeatures. Third, a **decoding and binding network** turns the features into the sound of the past tense.","children":[]},{"text":"The learning happens in the modifiable connections. They store the mapping from present to past. Since one network handles every verb, it has to explain both the regular pattern and the exceptions.","children":[]}],"m3-morphology-p010":[{"text":"Rumelhart and McClelland wanted their model to copy **U-shaped learning** in children. This is because it was one of the first learning models of a language process, so it aimed at how children develop.","children":[]},{"text":"U-shaped learning happens in three steps. First, children learn some frequent irregular verbs early, like run-ran. Then, on their own, they start saying **regularizations** like runned. A regularization is putting -ed on an irregular verb. Finally, they go back to saying ran.","children":[]},{"text":"It is called U-shaped because children get worse before they get better. Performance goes down and then comes back up.","children":[]},{"text":"Since the child goes right, then wrong, then right again, a theory has to explain the whole path.","children":[]}],"m3-morphology-p011":[{"text":"U-shaped learning matters to the **Words and Rules** account because each part of the U is a different stage. Each stage uses a different process.","children":[]},{"text":"This is because early correct forms are just **imitations**. The child copies ran without using adult mechanisms.","children":[]},{"text":"Next, the child learns the rule and uses it on every verb. The rule is the child's **best hypothesis** for all items. So the child starts saying runned.","children":[]},{"text":"Over time, the child learns the exceptions **associatively**, one by one. They become part of **lexical knowledge** (the child's stored word knowledge). The **blocking process** also develops. Blocking means a stored irregular stops the regular form from being used. So ran blocks runned.","children":[]},{"text":"Since the rule comes in before the exceptions are stored, the child goes through a dip and then recovers.","children":[]}],"m3-morphology-p012":[{"text":"Rumelhart and McClelland's model also gets worse before it gets better, with no rule at all. This is because its input changes over time. The **training set** is based on data about what children hear.","children":[]},{"text":"Early on, **irregular verbs** make up most of the input. The highest-frequency verbs tend to have irregular past tenses, like GIVE, TAKE, HAVE and GO. So the model learns these irregulars first.","children":[]},{"text":"Later, the vocabulary grows to include more regular verbs. The model **reorganizes** to pick up the regular pattern. That reorganizing **interferes** with some early irregulars.","children":[]},{"text":"For high-frequency irregulars, correct features dip for a short time and then climb slowly. Regular verbs climb fast and stay high. Early on, the regularized response briefly beats the correct irregular. With more training, the correct form wins and the regularized one fades.","children":[]},{"text":"Since the input shifts from irregulars to regulars, one network shows a U without a rule.","children":[]}],"m3-morphology-p013":[{"text":"The model could take a **wug test** using real English words. This is because it was trained on only a small number of verbs. So many real verbs were brand new to the model.","children":[]},{"text":"The researchers gave it unfamiliar low-frequency regular verbs. Many answers were correct regular forms, like shaped, sipped and typed. Some answers were strange, like stepted for step, squawked for squat and membled for mail.","children":[]},{"text":"The model got most **Wickelfeatures** right. It got .92 of them right for regulars and .84 for irregulars. Overall it got .91 right.","children":[]},{"text":"Getting features right is not the same as getting whole words right. A word with mostly correct features can still come out wrong. Since the scores count features, the model generalized well on features but could still produce odd words.","children":[]}],"m3-morphology-p014":[{"text":"Some of the model's answers changed consonants in ways people would not. This is because just four responses added or changed consonants, and they were strange. The model said maded for mate. It said squawked for squat. It said membled for mail. It said toureder for tour.","children":[]},{"text":"The model's authors admitted humans are unlikely to make these errors, especially the last two. They said most of these answers were **near threshold** (only weakly produced).","children":[]},{"text":"They suggested adding an **auto-associative network** among the output units. That network could clean up the output and make bizarre answers less likely. They had not built it yet.","children":[]},{"text":"Since people would never say membled or toureder, getting the learning curve right is not enough. The model also has to make words people would actually say.","children":[]}],"m3-morphology-p015":[{"text":"**Prince and Pinker (1988)** said the model mixes competing past tenses into bizarre hybrids. This is because it uses **feature-only representations** (representations made only of features). With features alone, it is hard to represent two things at once.","children":[]},{"text":"They gave a vision example. Features alone cannot tell a green circle near a red square from a red circle near a green square.","children":[]},{"text":"The past tense has the same problem. In the Rumelhart and McClelland model, subregularities and the regular rule all compete to turn on Wickelfeatures for the past form. For sing, features for sing, sang, sung, singed and sanged can all be on at once.","children":[]},{"text":"So the model does not put out one word. It puts out an unordered pile of features, and many contradict each other. That is how hybrids like toureder for tour and membled for mail appear. Since the features blend, the model cannot keep the competing answers separate.","children":[]}],"m3-morphology-p016":[{"text":"**Joanisse and Seidenberg (1999)** said sound supports regular patterns, while meaning gives extra help to irregulars. This is because their model links **speech input**, **semantics** (word meaning) and **speech output** in one system. Speech input and semantics both connect through a middle layer to speech output.","children":[]},{"text":"**Rule-like generalization** comes from **phonological representations** (the sound forms of words). The model learns the common sound pattern from present to past. That lets it extend the pattern to new words.","children":[]},{"text":"**Irregular** forms need more support from **semantics**. This is because an irregular does not follow the common sound pattern. So the model leans on what the word means to get it right.","children":[]},{"text":"Since both kinds of verb use the same system, regulars and irregulars differ only in how much they use sound and meaning.","children":[]}],"m3-morphology-p017":[{"text":"Joanisse and Seidenberg built their model to explain a patient **dissociation**. This is because some patients are worse at **irregular** past tenses, while other patients are worse at **nonword** past tenses.","children":[]},{"text":"The model has to produce both patterns from one system. A nonword has no stored meaning. So it depends on phonological generalization. An irregular does not fit the main pattern well. So it depends more on semantics.","children":[]},{"text":"If that is right, damaging phonology should hurt nonwords most. Damaging semantics should hurt irregulars most.","children":[]},{"text":"Each item type leans on a different part of the system. So damage to each part should give opposite profiles.","children":[]}],"m3-morphology-p018":[{"text":"**Broca's aphasia** patients have trouble with **regular morphology**, especially **nonwords**. This is because of injury to **left anterior** brain regions. These patients also have **poor speech production**.","children":[]},{"text":"Nonwords are the hardest. Examples are wug becoming wugged and blick becoming blicked. A nonword has no stored past tense, so the patient has to build it from the pattern.","children":[]},{"text":"Their scores follow a clear order. Irregulars are about 85 percent correct. Regulars are about 82 percent correct. Nonwords are about 69 percent correct.","children":[]},{"text":"The drop for nonwords is much bigger than the gap between irregulars and regulars. Since nonwords depend most on building a form from sound, this raises the question of whether the real problem is phonology.","children":[]}],"m3-morphology-p019":[{"text":"When the model's **phonology** is damaged, it makes the same pattern as Broca's patients. This is because the damage **reduced** the model's ability to compute past-tense forms.","children":[]},{"text":"**Nonwords** came out worse than regulars and irregulars. Model nonwords were about 72 percent correct. Patient nonwords were about 69 percent.","children":[]},{"text":"**Regulars** came out a little worse than irregulars, but only in the numbers. Model regulars were about 80 percent. Model irregulars were about 85 percent.","children":[]},{"text":"Familiar words still get support from their training. A new nonword has none of that support. So weak sound processing hurts nonwords the most.","children":[]},{"text":"Since the model had no rule to lose, a phonological problem can look like a morphology problem.","children":[]}],"m3-morphology-p020":[{"text":"Some patients have the opposite problem, and they are **poor on irregulars**. This is true of **Wernicke's** and **Alzheimer's** patients. It is also true of **anomic** patients (patients who have trouble finding words) with injury to **inferior or middle temporal** brain regions.","children":[]},{"text":"Their scores follow the reverse order. Irregulars are about 60 percent correct. Regulars are about 89 percent correct. Nonwords are about 84 percent correct.","children":[]},{"text":"This is the other half of the dissociation. Broca's patients do worst on nonwords. These patients do worst on irregulars. Since the two groups fail on opposite item types, a theory has to explain both.","children":[]}],"m3-morphology-p021":[{"text":"When the model's **semantics** is damaged, irregulars come out worst. This is because irregulars lean on meaning more than other verbs do. So losing meaning hurts them the most.","children":[]},{"text":"**Irregulars** were worse than regulars and nonwords. Model irregulars were about 62 percent correct. Alzheimer's patients were about 60 percent on irregulars.","children":[]},{"text":"**Nonwords** were a bit worse than regulars. Model nonwords were about 77 percent. Model regulars were about 83 percent. Nonwords are a bit worse because they have no training of their own.","children":[]},{"text":"Regulars and nonwords still get help from typical sound patterns. Since only meaning was damaged, the model matched these patients without deleting any list of exceptions.","children":[]}],"m3-morphology-p022":[{"text":"Two studies found that the patient pattern depends on sound and meaning, not on rules versus words.","children":[]},{"text":"**Bird, Patterson, Lambon-Ralph, McClelland and Seidenberg (2003)** tested **Broca's patients**. They controlled the regular and irregular items adequately for **phonology**. The gap between regulars and irregulars got **smaller**. This is because part of the gap came from sound differences between the items.","children":[]},{"text":"**Patterson, Lambon-Ralph, Hodges and McClelland (2001)** tested **semantic dementia** patients (people losing word meanings). They compared how bad each patient was on irregulars with how bad the semantic deficit was. The two **correlated strongly**. This is because irregulars depend on meaning, so they get worse as meaning is lost.","children":[]},{"text":"Since both effects line up with sound and meaning, the dissociation does not need separate rules and words.","children":[]}],"m3-morphology-p023":[{"text":"This account is similar to the **triangle model** of dyslexia. Sound supports generalization, and meaning supports odd forms.","children":[]},{"text":"**Phonological representations** support **generalization**. This is because generalization comes from **statistical learning** (picking up patterns from many examples) about the mapping from present to past. So if phonological representations are damaged, these generalizations get worse.","children":[]},{"text":"**Semantic representations** support **idiosyncratic** forms (forms particular to one word). This is because forms that break the overwhelmingly regular \"add -ed\" pattern need help from meaning.","children":[]},{"text":"Some irregulars can use **sub-patterns** shared with similar verbs. Those irregulars are likely to be **spared**. Since every form uses both systems, regulars and irregulars differ only in how much they use each one.","children":[]}],"m3-morphology-p024":[{"text":"**Specific language impairment (SLI)** is language development that is impaired, while \"normal\" performance and **nonverbal IQ** stay intact.","children":[]},{"text":"It affects **grammar** in two areas. This is because it hurts both **morphology** (how words are built) and **syntax** (how sentences are built).","children":[]},{"text":"It also affects things outside grammar. These are **phonology** (speech sounds), **reading**, **working memory** and **speech perception**.","children":[]},{"text":"Since SLI reaches past grammar, a theory that puts the whole problem in grammar has to explain these other problems too.","children":[]}],"m3-morphology-p025":[{"text":"Earlier claims said SLI is a problem with **learning rules**. This is because **past-tense deficits** are a **sine qua non** (essential feature) of SLI. Gopnik, van der Lely, Pinker, Ullman and Clahsen said the deficit was specific to rules.","children":[]},{"text":"A rule deficit predicts a clear pattern. People with SLI should have trouble with **regulars**. They should have trouble with **nonwords**. They should not have trouble with **irregulars**, because irregulars are stored words.","children":[]},{"text":"If that pattern held, SLI could mean that language acquisition needs **domain-specific** mechanisms (mechanisms used only for language). Since the claim depends on irregulars being fine, the real test is how people with SLI do on irregulars.","children":[]}],"m3-morphology-p026":[{"text":"People with SLI did badly on every kind of past tense, not just regulars and nonwords. This was the finding of **van der Lely and Ullman (2001)**, who compared an **SLI group** with **same-age controls**.","children":[]},{"text":"The SLI group was low on all three types. Regulars were about 22 percent correct. Irregulars were about 18 percent. Nonwords were about 7 percent.","children":[]},{"text":"The controls were much higher on all three types. Regulars were about 95 percent correct. Irregulars were about 88 percent. Nonwords were about 74 percent.","children":[]},{"text":"This is a problem for the rule account because irregulars were **not spared**. Irregulars were almost as low as regulars. Nonwords were still the hardest. Since every type was hurt, the deficit is broader than a rule deficit predicts.","children":[]}],"m3-morphology-p027":[{"text":"When the model's **phonology** was weakened, it learned every past-tense form poorly, and **nonwords** were worst. This is because weaker sound processing makes every form harder to learn.","children":[]},{"text":"With a **severe phonological deficit**, regulars were about 39 percent correct. Irregulars were about 17 percent, and nonwords were about 7 percent.","children":[]},{"text":"With a **weaker phonological deficit**, regulars were about 55 percent. Irregulars were about 38 percent, and nonwords were about 15 percent.","children":[]},{"text":"The **intact** model did much better. At the end of training, regulars were about 100 percent, irregulars about 94 percent and nonwords about 80 percent. At 700K iterations, regulars were about 90 percent, and irregulars and nonwords were both about 75.","children":[]},{"text":"The weakened models have the SLI order, with regulars best and nonwords worst. Since only phonology was weakened, no missing rule is needed.","children":[]}],"m3-morphology-p028":[{"text":"Phonology affects the learning of **all** past-tense forms. This is because every spoken past tense uses sound. So when phonology is weak, regulars, irregulars and nonwords all go down.","children":[]},{"text":"Phonology is **especially important** for **nonwords**. This is because a nonword has no training of its own. So the only way to make its past tense is to use a sound pattern.","children":[]},{"text":"Familiar words get extra help from what was learned about that specific word. So they lose less when phonology is weak.","children":[]},{"text":"Since nonwords have nothing else to lean on, they show the biggest deficit. Every other form still depends on phonology too.","children":[]}],"m3-morphology-p029":[{"text":"In this account there are no **special rules** for the past tense. There are only mappings between **phonology** and **semantics**. This is because the system learns how sound and meaning connect, instead of storing a rule. It is similar to the **triangle model** of reading.","children":[]},{"text":"There is also no **separate lexicon** that stores the exceptions. The words **rule** and **exception** only roughly describe what the system does.","children":[]},{"text":"Two findings back this up. **Irregular** forms are tied to **semantic processing**. **Wug test** performance is tied to **phonological processing**.","children":[]},{"text":"Since irregulars go with meaning and new words go with sound, one learning system can explain both without a rule or a list of exceptions.","children":[]}],"m3-morphology-p030":[],"m3-thought-p001":[],"m3-thought-p002":[{"text":"Linguistic relativity asks whether differences in language change how people organize concepts. This is because the Sapir and Whorf quotations say that **linguistic categories** (the word groups a language uses) shape how experience is interpreted. The strongest reading says language decides what a person can think. The weaker reading says habitual language use biases attention, memory, or preferred ways of describing things.","children":[]},{"text":"These two readings need different evidence. The quotations are broad ideas, not experiments.","children":[]},{"text":"**Influencing thought** (shaping how a person thinks) is different from **completely determining thought** (leaving no other option). This is because influencing only biases attention, memory or preferred descriptions, while determining would decide what a person can think.","children":[]}],"m3-thought-p003":[{"text":"Language differences can overlap with other differences between communities. This is because a difference between language communities can go along with factors other than linguistic structure. Sapir and Whorf should not automatically get the strongest reading of their quotations. In the first half of the twentieth century, linguistics was part of **anthropology** (the study of human cultures). So treating language as part of culture seemed natural then.","children":[]},{"text":"Testing linguistic relativity needs a narrower claim than saying each language creates a separate world. This is because **domains** (specific areas of experience) such as space and time let researchers test preferred ways of representing things. A precise study must identify which **cognitive process** (a mental operation such as remembering) is affected. It must also check which other explanations the comparison controls.","children":[]}],"m3-thought-p004":[{"text":"A location description needs a **frame of reference** (the set of directions used to say where something is). This is because saying where the ball is requires choosing what counts as left, front, or a stable direction. The ball can be described relative to the viewer, relative to the chair's own orientation, or by a direction that depends on neither. Different descriptions can pick out the same physical position. The object does not have to move for its description to change. This is because changing the reference changes the relation being described.","children":[]}],"m3-thought-p005":[{"text":"A description can use one of three frames, which are intrinsic, relative, and absolute. This is because an **intrinsic frame** (the object's own orientation) gives \"at the chair's front\". A **relative frame** (the observer's viewpoint) gives \"to the left of the chair from my viewpoint\". An **absolute frame** (a stable direction) gives \"north of the chair\". This is because north does not reverse when the observer turns around. Rotating the observer changes left and right, but it does not change north. So the frame has to be stated, since different frames pick out different directions. This classification comes from **Levinson and colleagues (2002)**.","children":[]}],"m3-thought-p006":[{"text":"Most languages describe nearby space from the speaker's own position. This is because left, right, in front, and behind all depend on where the speaker is. Tzeltal, a Western Mayan language spoken in Chiapas, does not do this. This is because Tzeltal commonly uses **absolute directions** where English speakers might use relative terms. A small number of other languages also use absolute directions, including some spoken in Bali and Queensland. Those languages are mostly unrelated to one another.","children":[]},{"text":"This raises a question about habits. This is because the question is whether habitual language use relates to nonverbal spatial coding. The comparison concerns preferred spatial descriptions.","children":[]}],"m3-thought-p007":[{"text":"**Tzeltal's uphill and downhill terms** work as an absolute system. This is because things are uphill or downhill from one another, not to the left or right of the speaker. These terms historically came from landmarks, but each use does not just point to the nearest visible hill. The direction is kept as a stable reference. So the speaker's current left and right do not set it again. This is why positions can be described consistently as the speaker turns. Where a term came from is a different property from whether it is absolute.","children":[]}],"m3-thought-p008":[{"text":"New Yorkers use **uptown** and **downtown** as if they were cardinal directions. This is because these terms only work inside Manhattan, so they are literally a landmark-based system. Manhattan is angled relative to magnetic north. This is because the city's conventional map orientation differs from magnetic north, so the subway map is off on north and south. A local convention is not automatically an absolute frame that works everywhere. This is because its usefulness depends on the landmark system being available.","children":[]}],"m3-thought-p009":[{"text":"**Majid and colleagues (2004)** used a non-linguistic test of relative and absolute coding of space. This is because the **chips task** rotates the participant 180 degrees between two tables. A participant sees a configuration, waits, and then chooses its match at the second table. A **relative response** keeps the arrangement with respect to the person's viewpoint. An **absolute response** keeps the arrangement's orientation in the environment. This is because after rotation the two responses point to different choices. Dutch speakers mostly gave relative responses. Tzeltal speakers gave more absolute responses. The pattern fits each group's preferred frame. It does not separate language from all other experience.","children":[]}],"m3-thought-p010":[{"text":"**Spatial inference** can combine relations learned in different displays. This is because participants learn one relation between two objects, rotate, and learn a second relation with a shared object. They then place one object relative to another using both relations. Relative and absolute coding lead to different placements. This is because the person's orientation has changed between the displays. The cube appears in both displays, so it links the cone and cylinder relations. The final placement separates a viewer-relative inference from one that keeps environmental direction. Dutch and Tzeltal patterns differ again. This is because each group leans toward the direction that matches its preferred frame.","children":[]}],"m3-thought-p011":[{"text":"Testing conditions may explain part of the language difference. This is because **Li and Gleitman** noted that the Tzeltal participants were tested outside, with visible and familiar landmarks. Typical psychology lab studies happen in windowless rooms. Landmarks can encourage landmark-based coding, so a windowless room makes viewer-relative descriptions more convenient. A language comparison can be **confounded** (mixed up with another difference) by a setting comparison. The critical contrast is access to spatial cues, not simply the label \"laboratory.\" Manipulating landmarks within English speakers tests whether the behavior can change without changing their native language.","children":[]}],"m3-thought-p012":[{"text":"English speakers strongly preferred **relative responses** when the blinds were down. This matches the Dutch speakers in earlier studies. With the blinds up and a view of the street, the preference is less pronounced. Outdoors, the preference is also less pronounced. This is because outside landmarks change which references are useful. The language stayed the same across these conditions. So task context can change the response, which challenges the idea that language alone fixes the frame. The change is a weakening, not a full switch to absolute coding.","children":[]}],"m3-thought-p013":[],"m3-thought-p014":[{"text":"If language groups are tested under different conditions, their difference may partly come from those conditions. This is because visible landmarks and familiarity can go along with native language in a cross-community study. So a controlled comparison must separate the environment from any effect of habitual linguistic coding. An uncontrolled comparison cannot tell these two effects apart.","children":[]},{"text":"A **confound** is a factor that changes along with the variable of interest. This is because it offers another explanation for the result. It is not just any feature that happens to be present in the experiment. The bias toward relative reasoning might be an **artifact** (a result produced by how the study is run).","children":[]}],"m3-thought-p015":[{"text":"Levinson and colleagues argued that the simplified task made the experimenter's purpose too obvious. This is because low memory demands and short time or distance between displays make the rotation easy to notice. Li and Gleitman's participants were Ivy League students. This is because those students are good at working out what the experimenter wants. Participants who work out the purpose can change their answers. This is because a **demand-characteristic explanation** (the idea that a study's apparent purpose shapes responses) can act apart from habitual spatial coding. So the response focuses on memory load and task transparency. It does not deny that landmarks can ever matter.","children":[]}],"m3-thought-p016":[{"text":"**Levinson and colleagues (2002)** re-ran the animals in a row experiment with Dutch college students. They added a fourth animal and a longer delay to raise memory load. They also increased the distance between the stimulus and response tables. This is because more distance makes the rotation less obvious. Under these conditions, the bias toward relative framing remained. So the environmental effect depends on how the task is arranged. Each change has a purpose. More animals and a longer delay tax memory, and the separated tables lower how obvious the rotation is.","children":[]}],"m3-thought-p017":[{"text":"Memory can make the relative preference harder to interpret. This is because the relative bias is **robust** across environments when memory matters and the experimenter's interest is not obvious. The relative preference could still come from a verbal memory. This is because remembering the task can use verbal strategies as well as spatial ones. Researchers need to ask how the sequence is stored during the delay. This is because the same final choice can come from a verbal or a spatial memory. So memory muddies the waters, because people may solve the task partly with verbal strategies.","children":[]}],"m3-thought-p018":[{"text":"Participants might remember the animals as a verbal list, such as \"lion, tiger, bear\". This is because a list keeps the order of the animals. They could then reconstruct that order from left to right. That reconstruction can produce a response that looks relative. This is because a list keeps order but not compass direction. This strategy connects language to the task through **working memory**. The same choice can come from different internal codes. Left to right can feel natural for a sequence in time. This is because a later page links reading direction to how sequences are arranged in space.","children":[]}],"m3-thought-p019":[{"text":"**Metaphorical language** expresses one kind of experience using terms from another. This is because calling an argument ineffective describes how well it works, not a literal action with a blade. Physical and spatial terms can help express abstract relations. Time is useful for asking whether these habits go with nonlinguistic thinking. Whether these spatial phrases go with spatial thinking about time is an empirical question. A lyric from Brown and Byrd (1970) is one example of metaphor. It raises the question of how much of language can safely be described as non-metaphorical.","children":[]}],"m3-thought-p020":[{"text":"**Boroditsky and colleagues (2010)** compared spatial metaphors for time in English and Mandarin. English and Mandarin both use horizontal spatial language for time. Mandarin also often uses vertical terms. An earlier event can take an \"up\" term, and a later event can take a \"down\" term. These expressions are the starting observation. A behavioral task is needed to test whether the vertical contrast goes beyond words. The \"up\" in an earlier month is a temporal use. It does not claim that the month occupies a physical location.","children":[]}],"m3-thought-p021":[{"text":"**Boroditsky (2001)** ran a priming experiment on time judgments. Participants first process a horizontal or vertical spatial relation. They then answer whether one event is earlier or later. English speakers respond faster after horizontal primes than after vertical primes. Mandarin speakers have the opposite pattern in the comparison tested. Short training on vertical metaphors for time changes the pattern for English speakers. This suggests the pattern depends on experience, not on a fixed split between language groups. Lower reaction times mean faster processing. So horizontal and vertical results should be compared within each group.","children":[]}],"m3-thought-p022":[{"text":"**Casasanto and Bottini (2005)** offer reading direction as another explanation. This is because reading and writing habits can also organize sequences in space. Differences attributed to temporal metaphors may go along with whether text is read left to right, right to left, or vertically. Casasanto and Bottini use differences in text orientation and arrangement preferences to support this idea. Language groups can bundle language and literacy experience together. So reading direction has to be measured or controlled. The diagrams separate the starting side from the line direction. The table lists several arrangement patterns within the groups, so individual experience matters.","children":[]}],"m3-thought-p023":[{"text":"**Boroditsky and colleagues (2010)** excluded Chinese readers who reported reading top to bottom. This is because the test checks whether the vertical effect is explained entirely by that reading experience. English speakers have an advantage for a left-is-earlier horizontal mapping. Mandarin speakers have an advantage for a top-is-earlier vertical mapping in the comparison tested. The vertical effect stayed, which weakens that reading explanation. So reading top to bottom cannot fully explain the vertical effect. Lower reaction times mean a mapping advantage.","children":[]}],"m3-thought-p024":[{"text":"**Casasanto and Bottini (2010)** had participants sort events as past or future by pressing colored buttons. The mapping from color to category is reversed across blocks. So the past response is on the left half the time and on the right the other half. With standard text reading, responses are faster when past maps left and future maps right. This is because the response is about time, not an explicit spatial judgment, so the effect reveals an implicit spatial association. Color alone cannot explain the pattern. This is because the two mappings are **counterbalanced** (each mapping is used in turn across blocks). The effect concerns response time across mappings. It does not change which events count as past or future.","children":[]}],"m3-thought-p025":[{"text":"Short-term reading experience can change the link between space and time. This is because **Casasanto and Bottini (2010)** had participants read reversed text and then do the past and future button task. By the second mirror-reading block, the compatibility effect reverses. Responses are then faster when earlier events map to the right and later events map to the left. A flexible temporal mapping is hard to explain as a fixed result of grammar alone. This is because recent perceptual and reading experience also contributes to performance. The first block keeps the usual advantage, while the second favors the opposite mapping. So each block should be compared on its own colors before overall times are compared.","children":[]}],"m3-thought-p026":[{"text":"**Pullum (1989)** criticized how an account of different word roots became an exaggerated story about language and thought. His critique points against exaggerated claims about language and thought. Claims about a language's vocabulary are not automatic evidence for cognitive limits. This is because languages can express distinctions through separate roots, derived words, or phrases. Counting one form without the others can misrepresent a language's expressive capacity. A reliable claim needs an accurate linguistic description and an appropriate behavioral test. Languages can package the same distinctions in different ways. Roots, derived forms, and phrases are different, so simple vocabulary counts can mislead.","children":[]}],"m3-thought-p027":[],"m3-modalities-p001":[],"m3-modalities-p002":[{"text":"Sign language and Braille are really different cases. This is because a **signed language** (a natural language with its own linguistic structure) is a language in its own right. **Braille** (a system that lets blind people read a spoken language) is a way to read a language that sighted people also read.","children":[]},{"text":"The two cases are still grouped together. This is because both reveal the **plasticity** (the ability of sensory systems to change with experience). Both also show how the brain gets organized differently when language input comes through a different **modality** (the sense that carries the input). The linguistic status of the two cases still differs.","children":[]}],"m3-modalities-p003":[{"text":"Sign languages differ from spoken languages in four ways. This is because sign uses a different **modality** (the sense that carries the language), which is vision instead of hearing. Sign also uses different **articulators** (the body parts that produce the language), which are the hands, face and upper body. Sign also uses **space** in a linguistic way, and some signs are **iconic** (their form looks like their meaning).","children":[]},{"text":"**Emmorey and colleagues (2004)** list these differences. Iconicity is easy to exaggerate. This is because it looks strongest when a single sign is studied with its **gloss** (the translation word for that sign). Once the translation is known, a resemblance looks obvious. A learner still has to learn the forms and structure of the language.","children":[]}],"m3-modalities-p004":[{"text":"Fluent signing uses the hands, face, upper body and eye contact together. This is because the linguistic signal is spread across all of these articulators, not one hand movement at a time. **Eye contact** is also part of the interaction.","children":[]},{"text":"Fluent signing is also fast and fluid. This is because signs run into each other as one continuous stream. Since the stream is continuous, a learner may find it hard to **segment** (find where each word begins and ends). Fluent speech is also continuous, so it has the same problem.","children":[]},{"text":"The thing to study is coordinated language production. This is because one isolated hand pose misses the coordination that makes the signal work.","children":[]}],"m3-modalities-p005":[{"text":"Signs can differ in ways that change their meaning, just as speech sounds can. The main features are **hand configuration** (the shape of the hand), **location** (where the sign is made) and **movement** (how the hand moves). **Emmorey and colleagues (2003)** name these features.","children":[]},{"text":"The signs for \"please\" and \"sorry\" differ, and so do the signs for \"onion\" and \"apple.\" This is because changing one feature can turn one sign into a different sign. Visual similarity alone does not decide whether two forms are the same sign.","children":[]},{"text":"A feature is **contrastive** (changing it changes the sign's identity). This is because a changed feature changes which sign is meant. Handshape, location and movement are separate dimensions to check. Since they are separate, each one has to be checked on its own.","children":[]}],"m3-modalities-p006":[{"text":"Researchers asked whether **categorical perception** (a pattern where a gradual change is sorted into separate categories) applies to these sign features. The answer depends on the kind of change. This is because the test changes hand configuration in steps, from one form toward another.","children":[]},{"text":"Observers identify each form and also tell pairs of forms apart. This is because the two responses together show whether the change is sorted into categories. One continuum crosses a **phonemic** change (a difference that changes which sign is meant), between \"please\" and \"sorry.\" The other continuum makes an **allophonic** change (a change that does not change the sign's identity).","children":[]},{"text":"Comparing equal steps is more informative than comparing only the endpoints. This is because a step that crosses a linguistic boundary can be compared with an equal step inside one category. So the test can separate a physical change from a change in category.","children":[]}],"m3-modalities-p007":[{"text":"Deaf signers show a sharper category boundary for the phonemic change than for the allophonic change. For the phonemic change, **identification** (labeling each form as one sign or the other) shifts steeply between stimuli 4 and 6. Discrimination (telling two forms apart) peaks at that same boundary.","children":[]},{"text":"This is because pairs of forms that cross the boundary are easier to tell apart. For the allophonic change, identification is much shallower. This is because many more forms are ambiguous. There is no peak in discrimination.","children":[]},{"text":"So the perceptual pattern follows the linguistic contrast, not the amount of visible change. A sharp identification shift with a discrimination peak supports categorical perception. Identification alone would be a less complete test.","children":[]}],"m3-modalities-p008":[{"text":"Sign language uses space to show how words in a sentence relate. This is because a **classifier sign** (a sign whose form stands for a kind of object) is made at a location. The relative position of two objects is given by where that classifier sign is made.","children":[]},{"text":"Understanding sentences in sign requires keeping track of where signs were made. This is because each object introduced in space has to be remembered at its location. Later signs relate back to those locations. So spatial processing helps comprehension, and it is not just background vision.","children":[]}],"m3-modalities-p009":[{"text":"Brain activity in sign comprehension is similar to spoken language in some ways and different in others. This is because signing is **left lateralized** (its activity is concentrated in the left hemisphere), as spoken language is. Classical language areas are also active, including **Broca's area** (the inferior frontal region) and **Wernicke's area** (the posterior superior temporal region).","children":[]},{"text":"Signing also activates **visual areas** and the **superior parietal** region. This is because signing makes visual and spatial demands. These are classic spatial areas.","children":[]},{"text":"**Campbell, MacSweeney and Waters (2008)** compared deaf signers, hearing signers and hearing people who watched audio-visual speech. Hearing signers are useful. This is because signing experience is not limited to deaf people. Overlap in regions does not require identical activation everywhere.","children":[]}],"m3-modalities-p010":[{"text":"**Bavelier and colleagues (2001)** asked whether deafness or experience with sign changes spatial attention. They compared hearing controls, deaf signers and hearing signers. Participants monitored for brief changes in **luminance** (brightness) or **velocity** (speed of motion) of dots. Attention was aimed at the center of the screen or at the periphery.","children":[]},{"text":"Deaf participants had an advantage when attention was aimed at the periphery. Hearing signers did not show that advantage. So the enhancement goes with deafness, not with signing experience.","children":[]},{"text":"Hearing signers help separate sign experience from the lack of auditory input. This is because a deaf versus hearing comparison alone would bundle the two together. If all signers had the advantage, signing alone would be the stronger explanation.","children":[]}],"m3-modalities-p011":[{"text":"Learning sign language and being deaf have different effects on visual processing. Learning sign language moves the **laterality** (which side of the brain does the work) of motion perception from right to left. Deaf and hearing signers both show this leftward shift compared with hearing nonsigners.","children":[]},{"text":"Enhanced peripheral attention is found only in deaf individuals. This is because hearing signers do not show it. One proposed reason is that hearing people depend more on auditory cues at the far periphery.","children":[]},{"text":"One proposed reason for the leftward shift is that motion perception needs to talk to left-lateralized language networks. So the two effects should not be merged into one advantage, because they track different aspects of experience.","children":[]}],"m3-modalities-p012":[{"text":"Comparing sign with **tic-tac** helps separate language processing from hand movement processing. This is because tic-tac is an outdated gesture system used for horse-racing odds in the UK. Both tasks involve meaningful, sequenced hand movements. So the control shares movement demands with sign.","children":[]},{"text":"The control is not a natural language. This is why the remaining difference is informative. A difference between sign and tic-tac says more about language than a comparison with no movement. The comparison does not assume every meaningful gesture is a language.","children":[]}],"m3-modalities-p013":[{"text":"Sign produces stronger activity around Wernicke's area than tic-tac does. **Campbell, MacSweeney and Waters (2008)** reported this comparison. Wernicke's area is in the posterior superior temporal region. This is why the comparison points to language processing rather than hand movement alone.","children":[]},{"text":"Shared hand movement does not explain the whole neural response to signing. This is because the frontal activation lies above Broca's area. So it should not be called a precise Broca's effect. The posterior temporal difference is the central result. The comparison weakens an explanation based only on hands moving through space.","children":[]}],"m3-modalities-p014":[{"text":"Signed languages have phonological structure. Native speakers show categorical perception for some contrastive features. Learning a signed language causes some brain reorganization, especially in motion processing.","children":[]},{"text":"Attention to the visual periphery matters for both hearing and deaf signers. Only deaf signers show enhanced peripheral attention.","children":[]},{"text":"Compared with organized hand movements that are not language, signed sentences activate the **posterior superior temporal gyrus** (Wernicke's area). This is because that region is tied to language.","children":[]},{"text":"A full account has to separate shared language organization from sensory adaptation. This is because neither \"sign is just gesture\" nor \"modality makes no difference\" fits the evidence.","children":[]}],"m3-modalities-p015":[{"text":"Understanding a signed spatial description can require a perspective change. A room can be described from the **speaker's perspective** (the signer's own viewpoint) or the **addressee's perspective** (the viewpoint of the person being addressed). Comprehension was better for descriptions from the speaker's perspective. This is true even though the listener has to make a mental transformation.","children":[]},{"text":"The most natural representation is not always the one with no spatial transformation. The two signing sequences describe the same table location. The difference is whose perspective organizes the description, not where the table is.","children":[]}],"m3-modalities-p016":[{"text":"Braille reading is much like print reading in many ways, but its symbols are built for touch. This is because **Braille symbols** are optimized for **tactile discrimination** (telling symbols apart by touch). Reading Braille by sight is really hard. Fingers take in letters one at a time.","children":[]},{"text":"Braille is a way to read written language. This is because it is not a separate natural language with its own grammar. This makes Braille useful for asking how reading networks work when input comes through touch.","children":[]},{"text":"Sequential tactile sampling is a processing constraint. This is because fingers take in letters one at a time.","children":[]}],"m3-modalities-p017":[{"text":"Reading Braille activates visual cortex in blind people. The earlier the sight is lost, the more extensive the visual activity to touch. Early-blind participants show stronger involvement of the **visual word form area** (a region involved in visual word processing) than late-blind participants.","children":[]},{"text":"A region's usual sensory label does not completely decide what it can process. This is because visual cortex can take part in reading even though the input is touch. Early- and late-blind groups give a comparison of developmental sensory history.","children":[]},{"text":"Compare how much activation there is. Do not assume that every colored spot has the same function.","children":[]}],"m3-modalities-p018":[{"text":"Sighted adults who wore blindfolds for five days learned Braille more quickly than a control group. **Merabet and colleagues (2008)** asked sighted adults to wear blindfolds for five days while they trained to read Braille. The control group trained without the same continuous removal of visual input. This suggests that removing competing visual input can affect tactile learning over a short time.","children":[]},{"text":"The study tests a question about **competition** (inputs competing for the same brain areas). The question was whether competition could block hearing people from building strong peripheral attention. Because these participants were sighted adults, the study tests a different question from early blindness.","children":[]},{"text":"Cross-modal changes do not have to be limited to early development. This is because a short-term change can reveal **latent** (hidden) contributions to tactile processing. Comparing the groups separates training effects from the extra effect of continuous blindfolding.","children":[]}],"m3-modalities-p019":[{"text":"Visual cortex responses to Braille developed during blindfolding and reversed after the blindfold was removed on Day 6. This is because removing visual input lets tactile activity become more evident. Over the course of the experiment, sighted blindfolded participants began to show activity in **primary visual cortex** (the first cortical area for vision). They showed this activity while reading Braille.","children":[]},{"text":"The change was rapid and reversible. This is because the pattern fits **unmasking** (existing inputs becoming visible) rather than building a new pathway in a few days. Restoring vision shifts the balance again.","children":[]},{"text":"Day 6 is the post-blindfold condition. It is not just another day of blindfolding. The comparison covers Days 1, 3, 5 and 6.","children":[]}],"m3-modalities-p020":[{"text":"Disrupting visual cortex with **TMS** (transcranial magnetic stimulation, a method that briefly interferes with brain activity) impaired reading in the blindfolded group only. On Day 5, error rate rose under 1 Hz TMS compared with baseline and with sham (fake) stimulation. The nonblindfolded group did not show the same selective disruption. Solid symbols mark the blindfolded participants.","children":[]},{"text":"The vertical axis is error rate, so an upward change means worse performance. Interfering with a region and changing behavior is stronger evidence than activity alone. This is because it shows the region contributes to the task. The effect is no longer present in the same way after blindfold removal.","children":[]},{"text":"One proposed explanation is that visual cortex always gets weak tactile input. This is because that input is too weak to compete with the stronger and more consistent visual input. So a short removal of visual input can unmask tactile responses in visual cortex.","children":[]}],"m3-modalities-p021":[{"text":"Language circuitry stays resilient across sensory modalities. This is because classical language areas are active for sign, even though its input is very different. Braille reading seems to engage networks that are important for processing text.","children":[]},{"text":"Brain organization is shaped over development by competition between inputs. This is because when auditory input is unavailable, putative (assumed) auditory areas are used to process sign. Visual attention is also enhanced at the periphery. When an input is absent or reduced, other information can make a larger contribution.","children":[]},{"text":"The same kind of competition appears in the **Competition Model** for second language learning. Distinguish the language-network overlap from the visual-attention and tactile-recruitment findings, since they come from different experimental comparisons.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p001":[],"m3-review-p002":[{"text":"English spelling is hard to sound out, so English is called a **deep** script. This is because English has many irregular spellings. **Orthographic depth** (how consistently spelling matches pronunciation) is the name for this difference between scripts. A deep script has many irregular spellings. A **shallow** script has regular ones.","children":[]},{"text":"Other scripts are much more regular. **Finnish** and **Korean** are very shallow scripts. **Pinyin** for Mandarin and **French** are relatively regular too.","children":[]},{"text":"A **\"mispronouncing contest\"** made the point that **phonemes are not letters** and **letters are not phonemes**. People purposely mispronounced words like COLONEL and PARADIGM. They did this by saying the words the way the letters suggest. The real pronunciations do not follow the letters. Since the letters do not give the sounds, English readers have to learn many words through experience.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p003":[{"text":"Finnish is very easy to sound out, so Finnish children learn to **decode** (figure out a pronunciation from the written form) very quickly. This is because Finnish is a **transparent** script. The **Orthographic Depth Hypothesis** calls this **shallow**. Each letter matches more or less one **phoneme** (one sound category).","children":[]},{"text":"Double letters also have a fixed meaning. A doubled consonant is a **geminated** consonant (a consonant held longer). A doubled vowel is a long vowel.","children":[]},{"text":"Following a beginning Finnish lesson along with its text makes this clear. After about a minute, a listener gets the idea. Since each letter reliably gives one sound, what a child learns from one word works for many other words.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p004":[{"text":"Kids learn to decode more slowly in English than in most other alphabetic languages. This is because English is an **outlier** among languages that use the Roman alphabet. Its spelling is much less regular.","children":[]},{"text":"The evidence comes from reading **pseudowords** (made-up words that follow a language's spelling and sound patterns). A child has never seen a pseudoword before. So the child cannot read it from memory. The child has to sound it out from letter-sound patterns.","children":[]},{"text":"In **Grade 1**, kids learning many other languages come up with plausible pseudoword pronunciations at a **much higher rate** than English learners. Since English letter-sound patterns are less predictable, English learners have a harder time sounding out new words.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p005":[{"text":"Korean is even more regular than Finnish, because similar sounds get similar-looking symbols. This is because the shape of a Korean letter reflects how its sound is made. So sounds that are made in a similar way look alike on the page. The letters are also grouped into **syllable blocks** with a first, middle and sometimes last position.","children":[]},{"text":"The feature for **velar place of articulation** (sounds made with the back of the tongue at the soft palate) is the angular shape **ㄱ**. This is because all the velar consonants share that shape. The sounds **g**, **kk** and **k** all use it. Doubling the shape or adding a stroke tells them apart. Since related sounds share a shape, a reader can see the sound family in the letters.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p006":[{"text":"Being rare slows down naming much more for **exception words** than for **regular words**. This is called the **frequency by regularity interaction**, and it is a landmark finding in psycholinguistics.","children":[]},{"text":"This is because the two factors work together. **Naming latency** (how long it takes to start saying a written word) is the measure. A regular word follows the usual spelling-sound pattern. An exception word does not. For **common words**, exception and regular words both take about 540 ms. For **rare words**, exception words go up to about 580 ms. Regular rare words go up only to about 555 ms.","children":[]},{"text":"An **interaction** means the effect of one factor depends on the other factor. Since the exception cost shows up mainly for rare words, experience matters most when spelling and sound disagree. The finding has impacted other areas, most immediately **inflectional morphology**.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p007":[{"text":"One way to explain the interaction is that reading uses **two separate processes**, and the slower one only shows up for rare words and nonwords.","children":[]},{"text":"This is because the two processes have different jobs. The **sublexical process** is decoding, which sounds a word out from its spelling. The **lexical process** is knowing a particular word.","children":[]},{"text":"For **high-frequency (HF) words**, the lexical process is very fast. So decoding has little chance to affect the answer. For **low-frequency (LF) words**, the lexical process is slower. So the decoded pronunciation shows up. For **nonwords**, there is no stored word at all. So reading depends on decoding.","children":[]},{"text":"For a rare exception word, decoding gives the regular, wrong pronunciation. That wrong answer gets in the way of the right one. Since this conflict only happens when the lexical process is slow, regularity matters most for rare words.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p008":[{"text":"**Seidenberg and McClelland (1989)** built a **PDP model** that needs no separate rules and no separate lexicon. A PDP model is a network that learns connections between spelling and sound.","children":[]},{"text":"This is because of two different assumptions from dual-route models. First, **\"rules\"** are just generalizations from lots of words. They are not a separate process. Second, the **\"lexicon\"** is spread out across the system. Some whole-word information is stored in the **O to P mappings** (spelling to sound connections). Some is stored in other parts of the system.","children":[]},{"text":"The research strategy is also different. Dual-route models work backward from adult and **neuropsychological** data to figure out the parts of the system. The PDP approach explains reading with **domain-general learning mechanisms** (learning that is not special to reading). Since one learning system does everything, word knowledge and patterns live in the same place.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p009":[{"text":"The PDP model produced the **frequency by regularity interaction** without separate processes for frequency and regularity.","children":[]},{"text":"This is because both factors come from learning. **Regular words** match the patterns of many other words. So those other words help the model with them. **Exception words** do not match those patterns. So they get no help. **Frequency** affects how well a word is learned. A common word is practiced many times.","children":[]},{"text":"A common exception word is practiced so often that not matching the pattern hardly matters. A rare exception word gets little practice and no help from other words. Since a rare exception word has both problems, it is the hardest of all.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p010":[{"text":"A PDP model reads **nonwords** (made-up letter strings) with the same machinery it uses for real words. This is because nonwords are named with the **same set of weights** (learned connection strengths) used for words. So no separate rule system is needed.","children":[]},{"text":"The model sounds out a new nonword by **generalization**. This is because a nonword shares spelling patterns with many real words. Those same **statistical patterns** also make regular words easy. Since the patterns that help regular words also apply to new strings, one route can handle both words and nonwords.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p011":[{"text":"In practice, the **Seidenberg and McClelland** model was not very good at reading nonwords.","children":[]},{"text":"This is a classic case of **ambiguity in model evaluation** (not knowing what a bad result means). This is because a bad result has two possible causes. The **theoretical claim** might be wrong. That claim is that nonwords and exceptions can be read by the same associative mechanism. Or the problem might be something about **how the model was made**.","children":[]},{"text":"The way to tell is to fix the model and keep the theory. If the fixed model reads nonwords well, the theory survives. Since the later fix came from better **phonological representations** (how the model codes speech sounds), the problem was at least partly in the build.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p012":[{"text":"Better sound coding made a later model much better at nonwords. This is because nonword reading is tied to **phonological representations** (how speech sounds are coded). Many studies find that **decoding** (nonword reading) goes along with **phonological processing**.","children":[]},{"text":"The first model used odd sound codes. **Seidenberg and McClelland (1989)** used **Wickelfeatures**. Wickelfeatures did a poor job of coding which words sound alike. So the model's nonword reading suffered.","children":[]},{"text":"**Plaut and colleagues (1996)** improved the phonological representations. Their model did **better on nonwords**. They did not add a separate rule system. Since only the sound coding changed, the first model's failure came at least partly from how it was built.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p013":[{"text":"Poor sound coding hurts nonwords more than real words. This is because **generalization depends on similarity**, a principle that comes from **behaviorism**. A network handles a new item by how similar it is to items it learned.","children":[]},{"text":"Poor representations like Wickelfeatures fail to capture the **similarity space** (which words in the language sound alike). So learning does not carry over well to new forms.","children":[]},{"text":"Real words are protected. This is because the network has **word-specific information** (training on that exact word). That information does not depend entirely on generalization. So it holds up even with poor representations.","children":[]},{"text":"Nonwords are not protected. They have no word-specific information. So they depend completely on generalization. Since generalization is what breaks, nonwords suffer most.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p014":[{"text":"**Harm and Seidenberg (1999)** made a model's sound coding noisy. The model then ended up with the reading pattern of **developmental dyslexia** (a reading problem that appears during development).","children":[]},{"text":"This is because they changed only the amount of **noise** in the phonological representations. More noise makes the sound codes less reliable. The rest of the learning model stayed the same.","children":[]},{"text":"The noisy models matched two key findings. First, the bigger the **phonological deficit**, the worse the **phonological dyslexia** (trouble sounding out nonwords). Second, **word recognition** was relatively spared. Since only sound coding changed, a weak sound system alone can produce this pattern.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p015":[{"text":"**Pinker and Ullman (2002)** made a dual-route model for the past tense. It is similar to the reading dual-route model in one way and different in another.","children":[]},{"text":"The model takes a **word stem** (like walk or hold) and a **grammatical feature** (like past tense). The **lexicon** route looks the form up, so hold gives held. The **grammar** route adds a suffix, so walk gives walk plus -ed. A **blocking** link lets a found irregular stop the regular form.","children":[]},{"text":"The lexicon is part of **declarative memory** (memory for words and facts) in temporo-parietal cortex. The grammar is part of the **procedural system** (rules and skills) in frontal cortex and basal ganglia.","children":[]},{"text":"It is similar because both models split **word-specific knowledge** from a **general operation**. It is different because reading turns spelling into sound. This model instead changes a stem based on a grammatical feature.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p016":[{"text":"The past-tense dual-route model looks like the reading model, but it came from different reasons.","children":[]},{"text":"The reading model came from **neuropsychological double dissociations** (patients whose problems show up in opposite ways). This is because those patients suggested two separate routes.","children":[]},{"text":"**Pinker and colleagues** were motivated by other concerns. This is because **generative linguistics** at the time was mostly about categorical, symbolic, rule-based grammars. These rules often have **exceptions**. So a second route gives the exceptions somewhere to go. Since the two models came from different evidence, they look alike but were built for different reasons.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p017":[{"text":"The dual-process model gives exceptions their own process so that the grammar can stay neat. This is because linguists usually just left exceptions out of the grammar. They did not want to make the grammar messy with extra machinery. The dual-process model handles the exceptions and keeps the grammar elegant, symbolic and categorical.","children":[]},{"text":"The second process has all the properties the rule route does not have. It is **word-specific** (lexical). It is **sensitive to frequency** of occurrence. It is **sensitive to phonological similarity** (how alike words sound). Keep-kept, weep-wept and leap-leapt are a family of similar-sounding irregulars. Since the lexicon takes all the messy cases, the rule can stay simple.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p018":[{"text":"**Rumelhart and McClelland (1987)** said the past tense is **all associative learning**, with no separate rule.","children":[]},{"text":"This is because one trainable network learns the past tense for every verb. It turns the sound of a verb root into **Wickelfeatures** (the model's sound code). A **pattern associator** with changeable connections turns that pattern into a past-tense pattern. Then the output pattern is turned back into a past-tense form.","children":[]},{"text":"Regular forms are just a pattern the network picks up from many examples. Irregular forms are learned in the same network. Since one network learns both, there is no need for a separate -ed rule.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p019":[{"text":"Children get past tenses right, then get worse, then get better again, which is called **U-shaped learning**. Rumelhart and McClelland focused on this because their model was one of the first learning models of a language process.","children":[]},{"text":"This is because learning happens in three steps. First, children learn some common irregular verbs quite early, like run-ran. Then, on their own, children start producing **regularizations** (regular endings on irregular verbs), like runned. Eventually, they produce the correct forms again.","children":[]},{"text":"The name comes from the shape of performance over time. Performance goes down before it goes back up. Since the errors come after early success, a theory has to explain the whole path, not just the end.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p020":[{"text":"U-shaped learning matters to the **words and rules** approach because the approach explains it with separate stages and processes.","children":[]},{"text":"This is because each stage uses a different process. At first, early correct forms are just **imitations**. They do not use the same mechanisms as adult processing.","children":[]},{"text":"When the **rule** is first learned, children apply it to every verb. The rule is their best guess for all items. So they say runned.","children":[]},{"text":"Over time, children learn **exceptions** one by one, by association. These become part of their word knowledge. The **blocking process** also develops. So a stored irregular now stops the rule. Since the rule arrives before the blocking, there is a dip in the middle.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p021":[{"text":"Rumelhart and McClelland's single network also got worse before it got better, because its training input changed over time.","children":[]},{"text":"This is because the **training set** is based on data about the input to children. Early on, **irregular verbs** make up most of the input. The most common verbs tend to have irregular past tenses, like GIVE, TAKE, HAVE and GO. So the model learns these irregular forms first.","children":[]},{"text":"Later the vocabulary grows to include more **regular forms**. The model reorganizes to pick up those regular patterns. This **interferes** with some of the early-learned forms. So the model starts getting them wrong. With more training, the irregular forms come back. Since the dip comes from a change in input, no separate rule is needed.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p022":[{"text":"The model was trained on only a **small number of verbs**, so real English verbs could work as a **\"wug\" test**. A wug test asks for the past tense of a word the learner has not learned. This works because a real verb is still new to the model if the model never trained on it.","children":[]},{"text":"The test used **unfamiliar low-frequency regular verbs**. Many answers were correct, like guarded, mapped and typed. Some were odd, like shipped for shape.","children":[]},{"text":"The model got **92 percent** of **Wickelfeatures** right for regular verbs. It got 84 percent right for irregular verbs and 91 percent overall. Getting most features right is not the same as getting whole words right. Since the test words were new to the model, these scores measure how well it generalized.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p023":[{"text":"The model sometimes made strange past tenses that people would probably never make.","children":[]},{"text":"There were just four responses that added or changed consonants. The model gave maded for mate. It gave squawked for squat. It gave membled for mail. It gave toureder for tour.","children":[]},{"text":"The authors agreed that humans would be unlikely to make these errors, especially the last two. This is because these responses were mostly **near threshold** (weak, borderline outputs). The authors suggested adding an **auto-associative network** among the output units. That network could clean up the output and avoid bizarre answers. They had not yet built it. Since people do not make these errors, a good learning curve alone is not enough.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p024":[{"text":"**Joanisse and Seidenberg (1999)** made a newer model where sound handles regular patterns and meaning helps with irregular forms. Their model connects **speech input**, **semantics** (word meaning) and **speech output**.","children":[]},{"text":"This is because of two starting ideas. First, **rule-like generalization** is supported by **phonological representations** (sound codes). Second, **irregular forms** need more support from **semantics**. An irregular cannot be figured out from sound alone, so the meaning of that particular word helps.","children":[]},{"text":"Since both kinds of verbs use the same shared systems, there is no separate rule route.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p025":[{"text":"The **Joanisse and Seidenberg (1999)** model tries to explain why some patients fail on **irregular** past tenses while others fail on **nonword** past tenses. This pattern is a **dissociation** in neuropsychological data.","children":[]},{"text":"This is because each item type depends on something different. A **nonword** like wug has no meaning. So its past tense depends on **phonology** (sound). An **irregular** verb does not follow the usual pattern. So it depends more on **semantics** (meaning).","children":[]},{"text":"So damage to sound should hurt nonwords most. Damage to meaning should hurt irregulars most. Since one model has both parts, it can produce both patient patterns.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p026":[{"text":"Patients with **Broca's aphasia** have the most trouble putting **nonwords** into the past tense.","children":[]},{"text":"This group has injury to **left anterior** brain regions. They have **poor speech production**. They have problems with **regular morphology** (regular word endings), especially for nonwords like wug to wugged and blick to blicked.","children":[]},{"text":"The scores make this clear. Nonwords were the lowest, at about 69 percent correct. Regular verbs were about 82 percent correct. Irregular verbs were about 85 percent correct.","children":[]},{"text":"This is because a nonword has no stored past tense. So it has to be built from sound patterns. Since that depends on sound, this pattern points to a **phonological** problem.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p027":[{"text":"**Wernicke's** and **Alzheimer's** patients have the most trouble with **irregular** past tenses. So do **anomic** patients with injury to inferior or middle temporal brain regions.","children":[]},{"text":"The scores make this clear. Irregular verbs were about 60 percent correct. Regular verbs were about 89 percent correct. Nonwords were about 84 percent correct.","children":[]},{"text":"This is the reverse of the Broca's pattern. This is because irregulars need help from **semantics** (word meaning). These patients' problems fit damage to that support. Since nonwords and regulars do not need that help, they stay fairly good.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p028":[{"text":"The classical view splits aphasia into two neat types, which is an **attractive dichotomy**.","children":[]},{"text":"**Wernicke's aphasia** gives \"word salad\" or \"Jabberwocky,\" often with **neologisms** (made-up words). Syntax stays roughly intact. One patient said \"My wires don't hire right\" about his poor vision. Another called a fork \"a need for a schedule.\" So the speech flows but makes little sense.","children":[]},{"text":"**Broca's aphasia** gives **telegraphic** speech. Patients have trouble with **word order** and **grammatical particles** (small grammar words). Important **content words** are relatively spared. One patient described his hospital visit in bits like \"Two doctors ... and ah ... teeth.\" So the speech is halting but keeps the main words.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p029":[{"text":"Broca's own patients would not necessarily be called Broca's aphasics today. This is because they were probably unable to produce speech at all. The cause seems to have been **motor control** problems. Today they would be classified as having **apraxia of speech** (trouble planning speech movements).","children":[]},{"text":"The modern dichotomy treats **Wernicke's aphasia** as problems with **activation** in the lexicon, or maybe inhibition. It treats **Broca's aphasia** as problems with **grammar**. That is fairly far from how Broca described his cases.","children":[]},{"text":"His patient **Leborgne**, called \"Tan,\" could only produce one jargon syllable. He otherwise seemed to have all his senses. He even seemed to understand what was said to him. Since he could not speak at all, his problem looks like movement, not grammar.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p030":[{"text":"**Wernicke's aphasia** is a problem with activation in the **lexicon**, and **Broca's aphasia** is a problem with **grammar**. This dichotomy is appealing because it lines up with the sharp split between **grammar** and **lexicon** in late 20th century formal linguistics.","children":[]},{"text":"This is because that split starts with **Chomsky's** framing of the **computational problem of language learning**. People recognize a new sentence because their internal grammar generates it. The sentence does not have to match any familiar sentence.","children":[]},{"text":"Chomsky also argued that **syntax and semantics are independent**. **Chomsky (1955)** gave two word strings. Neither \"colorless green ideas sleep furiously\" nor \"furiously sleep ideas green colorless\" had come up in an English speaker's experience. Yet the first is grammatical and the second is not. Since grammar works apart from meaning, a grammar problem and a word problem seemed like separate aphasias.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p031":[{"text":"The speech production model also separates an arbitrary word level from a grammar level. These are the **functional level** and the **positional level**.","children":[]},{"text":"The levels go in order. The **message level** comes first and sets up concepts and events. The **functional level** brings up word meanings and semantic roles. The **positional level** puts word forms in a grammatical order. **Phonological encoding** and **articulation** come last.","children":[]},{"text":"The two middle levels work differently. This is because **lemmas** (word representations chosen at the functional level) have an **arbitrary** link to what they mean. The **syntactic trees** and **affixes** at the positional level come from abstract syntactic processes.","children":[]},{"text":"Words like HOUSEBOAT and DOGHOUSE seem to be a problem, because their meaning is not arbitrary. But speech errors like \"easy enoughly\" show **derivational morphology** happens at the positional level.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p032":[{"text":"Most languages describe nearby things **egocentrically** (from the speaker's own body), but Tzeltal uses **absolute directions** (directions that stay fixed when a person turns).","children":[]},{"text":"Egocentric words are left and right, in front and behind. They change whenever the speaker turns around.","children":[]},{"text":"**Tzeltal** is a Western Mayan language spoken in Chiapas. It does not describe nearby space this way. This is because Tzeltal uses absolute directions even for nearby things. A small number of other languages do too. They are mostly unrelated to each other and spread widely, for example in Bali and Queensland.","children":[]},{"text":"To an English speaker, being asked to \"raise your east hand\" sounds strange. Since English uses left and right, absolute terms feel unnatural to its speakers.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p033":[{"text":"In Tzeltal, things are **uphill** or **downhill** from each other, not to the left or right. This is because Tzeltal has a historically **landmark-based** system. The terms come from the slope of the land around Tenejapa.","children":[]},{"text":"\"Uphill\" and \"downhill\" are used as **cardinal directions** (fixed directions like compass directions). So Tzeltal is an **absolute** system. The terms do not just point to the nearest hill. They stay the same no matter which way the speaker faces.","children":[]},{"text":"**Levinson and colleagues (2002)** described this system. Since the directions do not depend on the speaker, they stay the same when the speaker turns.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p034":[{"text":"In a memory task with no words, **Dutch** speakers remembered space relative to their body, and **Tzeltal** speakers mostly remembered it in absolute terms. **Majid and colleagues (2004)** ran this **chips task**.","children":[]},{"text":"A person sees a card at Table 1 with a large dot and a small dot. After a 30-second wait, the person is **turned 180 degrees** and led to Table 2. Then the person picks the matching card out of four.","children":[]},{"text":"This is because turning around makes the two ways of remembering give different answers. A **relative** answer keeps the dots the same compared to the person's body. An **absolute** answer keeps the dots the same compared to the world, such as north.","children":[]},{"text":"Dutch answers were overwhelmingly relative. Tzeltal answers were mostly absolute. Since no language was used, the choices reflect how each group remembers space.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p035":[{"text":"Dutch and Tzeltal speakers also used their own spatial frames when combining two facts to place an object. **Majid and colleagues (2004)** ran this harder task.","children":[]},{"text":"At Table 1, the person sees a cube and a cone. The person turns 180 degrees to Table 2 and sees the cube with a cylinder. Then the person turns back to Table 1, where the cone stands alone. The task is to place the cylinder next to the cone.","children":[]},{"text":"This is because the two frames give different answers. With **relative** coding, the cube is right of the cone and the cylinder is right of the cube. So the cylinder goes right of the cone. With **absolute** coding, the cube is south of the cone and the cylinder is further south. So the cylinder goes south of the cone.","children":[]},{"text":"**Dutch** speakers gave relative answers. **Tzeltal** speakers gave absolute answers.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p036":[{"text":"**Li and Gleitman** argued that where people were tested, not just their language, might explain the Tzeltal results. They tried **inducing landmark-based similarity in English speakers**.","children":[]},{"text":"This is because the testing places were different. The **Tzeltal speakers** were tested outside, where familiar **landmarks** were visible. Typical psychology lab studies happen in **windowless rooms**.","children":[]},{"text":"Visible landmarks can push people toward absolute coding. A windowless room has no landmarks. So relative coding is easier there. Since the setting differed along with the language, the setting could explain part of the difference.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p037":[{"text":"English speakers used relative position less when they could see landmarks outside. **Li and Gleitman** found this by changing the view during testing.","children":[]},{"text":"With the **blinds down**, English speakers overwhelmingly used **relative** position. They acted like the Dutch speakers in earlier studies. With the **blinds up** and a view of the street, the relative preference was **weaker**. **Outdoors**, it was also weaker.","children":[]},{"text":"This is because only the setting changed. The language stayed the same. So the change must come from the available landmarks. A weaker preference is not a full switch to absolute coding. Since the same speakers changed with the setting, the testing environment also affects which spatial frame people use.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p038":[{"text":"**Levinson and colleagues** were not convinced by Li and Gleitman, because they thought the task made its purpose too easy to guess.","children":[]},{"text":"This is because Li and Gleitman made it too **transparent** that the task was about rotation. They **reduced the memory load**. They also cut down the **time and distance** between seeing the display and being tested. So people could figure out the trick instead of using their normal memory.","children":[]},{"text":"The participants were also **Ivy League students**. Such students are very good at figuring out what the experimenter wants and then doing it. Since these students could guess the goal, their answers might not show how they normally remember space.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p039":[{"text":"The question is whether signers show **categorical perception** (seeing a gradual change as a sudden jump between categories) for **hand configuration** in sign language.","children":[]},{"text":"This is tested with two step-by-step series of signs. The **phonemic** series changes the hand from the sign PLEASE to the sign SORRY. So it crosses a contrast between two different signs. The **allophonic** series changes the hand from open-N to closed-N in the sign SAY-NO-TO. So the hand changes, but the sign stays the same.","children":[]},{"text":"People **identify** each step and **discriminate** pairs of steps. Categorical perception predicts a sharp switch in identification. It also predicts the best discrimination right at that switch. Since only the phonemic series crosses a real contrast, only it should show this.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p040":[{"text":"Yes, deaf signers show **categorical perception** for the phonemic hand contrast, but not for the allophonic one. The curves have the familiar categorical shape.","children":[]},{"text":"This is because the **phonemic** series has a fairly sharp **boundary** between stimuli 4 and 6. That boundary lines up with the **peak** in the **discrimination function** (how well people tell two steps apart). So pairs that cross the boundary are the easiest to tell apart.","children":[]},{"text":"The **allophonic** series is different. Its identification function is **much shallower**. That means many more steps are ambiguous. Its discrimination function has **no peak**. Since only the meaningful contrast shows the pattern, the perception follows language, not just the look of the hand.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p041":[{"text":"Brain activity for sign language is a lot like brain activity for speech, with some differences. **Campbell, MacSweeney and Waters (2008)** compared deaf signers, hearing signers and hearing people perceiving audio-visual speech.","children":[]},{"text":"Sign is similar to speech. It is **left lateralized** (mostly on the left side of the brain). It uses the **classical language areas**. These are Broca's area in the inferior frontal region and Wernicke's area in the posterior superior temporal region.","children":[]},{"text":"Sign is also different. It uses more **visual areas** and fewer **auditory areas**. This is because sign is seen, not heard. It also uses **superior parietal** areas, which are classic spatial areas. Since sign uses space, those spatial areas get involved.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p042":[{"text":"Sign language is a real language with its own sound-like structure, and learning it changes the brain.","children":[]},{"text":"Sign language has **phonological structure** (organized form units). Native signers show **categorical perception** for some contrastive features.","children":[]},{"text":"Learning a signed language leads to some **brain reorganization**. The clearest example is the **lateralization of motion processing** (motion processing shifting to one side).","children":[]},{"text":"Attention to the edges of vision, the **periphery**, matters for both hearing and deaf signers. But only **deaf signers** show better processing of attention in the periphery. So that change comes with deafness.","children":[]},{"text":"Signed sentences activate **posterior STG** (Wernicke's area). This is compared with non-language hand movements that are also organized and sequenced. Since plain hand movements do not do this, the activation comes from language.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p043":[{"text":"Johnson and Newport see the **maturational state hypothesis** as fitting their data better. Both versions of the **critical period hypothesis** say people start life with a superior ability to learn languages.","children":[]},{"text":"The **exercise hypothesis** says the ability fades only if it is not used early. If it is used, later language learning stays intact for life. The **maturational state hypothesis** says the ability fades with age either way.","children":[]},{"text":"This is because **Johnson and Newport (1989)** studied people who already had a first language. So their ability had been exercised. They still learned a second language less well when they started later.","children":[]},{"text":"This does change how the **L1 critical period** data are read. People who learn a first language late never exercised the ability. So both versions predict they will do poorly. Since both predict it, L1 data cannot tell the versions apart. Early first-language experience still matters.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p044":[{"text":"**Mayberry, Lock and Kazmi (2002)** found that people who had no language early do much worse at learning a language later.","children":[]},{"text":"Many **deaf children** get no sign language input but have an otherwise normal upbringing. Kids aged 9 to 15 have a hard time learning **American Sign Language (ASL)** if it is their first language. Kids who went deaf after learning a **spoken language** do much better. These differences last into adulthood.","children":[]},{"text":"As adults, people with no early language recalled about 36 percent of ASL sentences. People with an early spoken language recalled about 78 percent. On English grammar judgments, people with no early language scored about 52 percent, near the 50 percent chance level. People with early signed or spoken language scored about 80 percent.","children":[]},{"text":"This is because an early language helps with learning any later language. Since early signed and spoken language helped equally, what matters is having some language early.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p045":[{"text":"The **semantic blocking effect** (slower naming when same-category objects are named together) probably does not come from objects looking alike.","children":[]},{"text":"The worry is that objects in the same category might look similar. Then the effect could come from **visual object recognition**, not from naming. That would be a **confound** (another possible cause).","children":[]},{"text":"This is ruled out in two ways. First, the stimuli were **normed**. On average, same-category objects were not more similar-looking than objects from different categories. Second, a **control experiment** grouped objects by **visual similarity** instead. It found different results. The visual blocking effect showed up in the **first block of trials**. The semantic effect only shows up after the first cycle. Since the timing differs, the semantic effect is not a visual effect.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p046":[{"text":"**Belke and colleagues** showed four objects at the same time but had people name them one after another.","children":[]},{"text":"This is because of a worry from another study. **Hermens and colleagues** showed people several objects at once. They found no blocking effect. So maybe blocking only happens when objects are seen one at a time.","children":[]},{"text":"In the Belke design, people viewed displays of **four items at once**. They said the four names **in order**. All the objects were visible together. But the names still had to be picked one after another. Since viewing and naming are now separated, the design can tell whether serial viewing matters.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p047":[{"text":"**Belke and colleagues** tracked eye movements to time each step of naming.","children":[]},{"text":"This is because the eyes and the voice do not move together. **Gaze duration** is how long the eyes stay on one object. **Eye-speech lag** is the time between the eyes leaving an object and starting to say its name.","children":[]},{"text":"A speaker often looks at the next object before saying the current name. So looking at an object and saying its name happen at different times. Since the two measures cover different times, each one can track a different stage of getting a word ready.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p048":[{"text":"The blocking effect shows up only in gaze duration, so it happens at **lemma selection** (choosing the word). The comparison is **homogeneous** blocks (same category) against **heterogeneous** blocks (mixed).","children":[]},{"text":"This is because each measure matches a different stage. **Overall speech duration** gets shorter over the experiment. It shows **no blocking effect**. It reflects the **gesture level** (moving the mouth).","children":[]},{"text":"**Gaze duration** shows a **blocking effect**, but only after the **first cycle**. It reflects lemma selection.","children":[]},{"text":"**Eye-speech lag** shows **no effect**. The numbers seem to go the opposite way, but this is **not significant**. Lag reflects **phonological encoding** (getting the sounds ready). Since only gaze duration shows the effect, the slowdown happens when the word is chosen.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p049":[{"text":"**McMurray, Tanenhaus, Aslin and Spivey (2003)** asked whether hearing a speech sound works in a **thresholded** way or a **cascaded** way when people recognize words.","children":[]},{"text":"In **thresholded** processing (the lumber mill), only a finished sound category gets passed on. In **cascaded** processing (the food web), partial information flows on before the decision is done.","children":[]},{"text":"They tested a **/b/ to /p/ continuum** (a series of steps from /b/ to /p/). The steps change **voice onset time** (VOT, the delay between releasing the consonant and starting voicing). People looked at a **visual world** display while their eyes were tracked. Each display had a target like a pear, a competitor like a bear and two fillers. This is because looks to the competitor show whether fine sound detail reaches the words.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p050":[{"text":"When people clicked on the bear or the pear, their choices formed a typical **step-like identification function**. **McMurray and colleagues (2003)** had people use the mouse to pick which of the pictures best matched what they heard.","children":[]},{"text":"This is because short VOTs sounded like /b/, so people chose the bear. Long VOTs sounded like /p/, so people chose the pear. The **category boundary** was at about 17.5 ms.","children":[]},{"text":"A step-like final choice can still come from **graded** (gradual, in-between) processing before it. Since the click only shows the final answer, the eye movements are needed to see what happened before it.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p051":[{"text":"Eye movements show that **ambiguity is gradient** (it comes in degrees). People look more at the **competitor** when the sound is close to the boundary.","children":[]},{"text":"This is because sounds near the boundary are less clear. When people chose the **bear**, sounds closer to the boundary drew more looks to the pear. When people chose the **pear**, sounds closer to the boundary drew more looks to the bear. VOT mostly changes how **long** the competitor stays active.","children":[]},{"text":"So small sound differences inside a category still affect **lexical activation** (which words become active). Since this detail gets through to the words, the results support **cascaded** processing.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p052":[{"text":"The closer a sound is to the boundary, the more people look at the competitor, on both sides of the boundary.","children":[]},{"text":"This is because the effect grows step by step. For **bear responses**, looks to the pear go up as VOT moves from 0 ms toward the boundary. For **pear responses**, looks to the bear go up as VOT moves from 40 ms down toward the boundary.","children":[]},{"text":"The looks are split into an **early window** (300 to 1100 ms) and a **late window** (1100 to 1900 ms). The pattern shows up in both windows. Since closeness to the boundary keeps the competitor active, the results support **cascaded** processing.","children":[]}]}};
